var tafsirData_28 = {
  "1": "\"Ta-Sin-Mim\" opens this new chapter as \"Ta-Sin\" opened the chapter before it. These are among the disconnected letters, \"huruf muqatta'ah,\" that begin several surahs. The Prophet recited them as letters. The Companions received them as part of the Quran. Reliable scholarship has not locked a single worldly meaning for them. Some said they are names of the surah, some said they are an oath, some said they point to the fact that the Book is made of the same letters the Arabs know, yet they cannot match it. The safest speech is that their full meaning is with Allah, and that they still do a clear work: they stop the listener at the door of a revelation that is not ordinary talk.\n\nThe extra letter \"Mim\" beside \"Ta-Sin\" marks this chapter as kin to the last and not a copy of it. Both will speak of Moses. This one will linger on his infancy, his mistake in the city, his flight, his hiring in Midian, and his standing before Pharaoh with Aaron. The letters are the threshold of that longer narration.\n\nBeginning with letters also resists the charge of \"ancient fables.\" A fable starts with \"once there was.\" This Book starts with sounds that belong to the Quran's own signature. Then it will say, these are the verses of the clear Book. The letters and the claim of clarity stand together. What begins in a way humans do not invent continues as a story told \"in truth for people who believe.\"\n\nNo Arabic script is needed to hold the letters. Their recited names are enough. The believer reads them, trusts them, and steps into the verses that follow with the same trust. That is already a small act of the submission the previous chapter commanded.\n\n'Ta-Sin-Mim' also functions as a doorway the listener must enter on foot. Recited letters give the assembly nothing to argue with, no claim to dispute, only sounds to receive or refuse, and that is precisely the test. By the time the story arrives, the audience has already shown, in how they took the letters, how they will take the verses that follow.",
  "2": "\"These are the verses of the clear Book.\" The same kind of sentence followed the letters in the previous chapter. \"These\" points to what is being recited. \"Verses\" are \"ayat,\" signs as well as sentences. \"The clear Book,\" \"al-kitab al-mubin,\" is the Quran as a writing that makes things plain.\n\nClarity here does not mean that every secret of the unseen is dated. The last chapter said none knows the unseen except Allah. Clarity means the guidance is not riddled on purpose. Commands, stories, and arguments can be understood by people who believe and think.\n\nRepeating this line after another set of letters teaches that each surah shares the same source and the same quality. A new chapter is not a new religion. It is more of the clear Book. The coming story of Moses and Pharaoh is therefore not entertainment. It is verses, signs, part of the Book that clarifies.\n\nMecca accused the Prophet of copying storytellers. A storyteller does not introduce his night-tale as \"verses of the clear Book.\" That title makes a claim of revelation and of moral transparency. The next verse will state the claim even more tightly: We narrate to you part of the story of Moses and Pharaoh in truth for people who believe. The clear Book is about to prove its clarity by telling a known history with a purpose.\n\n'Clear' also keeps its Quranic modesty. The Book clarifies what matters for guidance, worship, and the meeting with Allah, and it says plainly when a thing is known only to Him, as the last chapter said of the Hour's date. A book that faked omniscience would be suspect; one that marks its own boundaries is claiming a different kind of honesty, and calls it clarity. Believers have always found that honesty part of the Book's light: it never asks trust by hiding what it does not know.",
  "3": "“We narrate to you ˹O Prophet˺ part of the story of Moses and Pharaoh in truth for people who believe.”” opens the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the narration: or \"natloo 'alayka min naba'i Moosa wa Fir'awna bil-haqqi li-qawmin yu'minoon,\" and the commentators note the narration, for the Lord narrates the story in the truth, and the verse is the record of the narration, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the truth is the assurance, and the belief is the audience, and the verse is the record of the narration, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the narration is the truth and the belief is the audience, and the verse is the record of the narration, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the narration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the stories of the Quran are the truth, and the one who believes is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose faith the Lord accepts, and the narration of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must receive, and the believer who reads the narration is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the narrative: the story is the true, and the believer who reads it is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the narration is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must receive. The verse is the record of the narration, and the teaching of the faith that the truth produces.",
  "4": "“Indeed, Pharaoh ˹arrogantly˺ elevated himself in the land and divided its people into ˹subservient˺ groups, one of which he persecuted, slaughtering their sons and keeping their women. He was truly one of the corruptors.”” records the tyranny, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the tyranny: or \"inna Fir'awna 'ala fi al-ardi wa ja'ala ahlaha shiya'an yastad'ifu ta'ifatan minhum yuthabbihu abna'ahum wa yastahyee nisa'ahum innahu kana mina al-mufsideen,\" and the commentators note the tyranny, for Pharaoh elevated and persecuted, and the tyranny is the corruption, the oppression of the people, and the verse is the record of the tyranny, the corruption that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the slaughter is the tyranny, and the verse is the record of the tyranny, the corruption of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the tyranny is the corruption and the slaughter is the oppression, and the verse is the record of the tyranny, the corruption that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the tyranny, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the tyranny of the oppressor is the thing that the Lord answers, and the one who is oppressed is the one whose deliverance the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the tyranny of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the oppression is the thing that the Lord judges, and the believer who reads the tyranny is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the corruption of the tyranny: Pharaoh oppressed and was drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the tyranny is the teaching that the oppression is the thing that the Lord judges. The verse is the record of the tyranny, and the teaching of the trust that the oppression requires.",
  "5": "“But it was Our Will to favour those who were oppressed in the land, making them models ˹of faith˺ as well as successors;” records the will, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the will: or \"wa nureedu an namunna 'ala allatheena istud'ifoo fi al-ardi wa naj'alahum a'immatan wa naj'alahumu al-waritheen,\" and the commentators note the will, for the Lord willed the favour of the oppressed, and the will is the decree, the promise of the elevation, and the verse is the record of the will, the decree that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the favour is the mercy, and the succession is the reward, and the verse is the record of the will, the decree of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the will is the decree and the favour is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the will, the decree that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the will, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the favour of the Lord is the promise, and the one who is oppressed is the one whose elevation the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the will of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the elevation is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the will is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the promise of the Lord: the oppressed were elevated, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the will is the teaching that the elevation is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the will, and the teaching of the hope that the promise carries.",
  "6": "“and to establish them in the land; and through them show Pharaoh, Hamân, and their soldiers ˹the fulfilment of˺ what they feared.”” completes the will, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the will: or \"wa numakkina lahum fi al-ardi wa nuriya Fir'awna wa Hamana wa junoodahuma minhum ma kanoo yahtharoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord willed the establishment and the exposure, and the completion is the decree, the promise of the triumph, and the verse is the record of the completion, the decree that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the establishment is the reward, and the exposure is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the decree of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the decree and the establishment is the reward, and the verse is the record of the completion, the decree that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the establishment of the Lord is the reward, and the one who trusts is the one whose establishment the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the establishment is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the establishment of the Lord: the oppressed were established, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose establishment the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the establishment is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the promise produces.",
  "7": "“We inspired the mother of Moses: “Nurse him, but when you fear for him, put him then into the river, and do not fear or grieve. We will certainly return him to you, and make him one of the messengers.”” records the inspiration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the inspiration: or \"wa awhayna ila ummi Moosa an ardi'eehi fa-itha khifti 'alayhi fa-alqeehi fi al-yammi wa la takhafee wa la tahzanee inna raddoohu ilayki wa ja'iloohu mina al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the inspiration, for the Lord inspired the mother, and the inspiration is the guidance, the command of the trust, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the guidance that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the command is the trust, and the promise is the hope, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the guidance of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inspiration is the guidance and the promise is the hope, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the guidance that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inspiration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the trust of the Lord is the thing that saves, and the one who trusts is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who casts the affair to the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the inspiration to the mother is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the path of the safety, and the believer who reads the inspiration is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the mother: the child was cast and saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the inspiration is the teaching that the trust is the safety. The verse is the record of the inspiration, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance produces.",
  "8": "“And ˹it so happened that˺ Pharaoh’s people picked him up, only to become their enemy and source of grief. Surely Pharaoh, Hamân, and their soldiers were sinful.”” records the pickup, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the pickup: or \"faltaqatahu alu Fir'awna li-yakoona lahum 'aduwwan wa hazanan inna Fir'awna wa Hamana wa junoodahuma kanoo khati'een,\" and the commentators note the pickup, for the people of Pharaoh picked the child, and the pickup is the providence, the arrangement of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the pickup, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the enemy is the irony, and the verse is the record of the pickup, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pickup is the providence and the enemy is the irony, and the verse is the record of the pickup, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pickup, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the providence of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who is arranged is the one whose affair the Lord guides, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the pickup of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the pickup is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: the child was picked and became the enemy, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the pickup is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the pickup, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "9": "“Pharaoh’s wife said ˹to him˺, “˹This baby is˺ a source of joy for me and you. Do not kill him. Perhaps he may be useful to us or we may adopt him as a son.” They were unaware ˹of what was to come˺.”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the plea: or \"wa qalati imra'atu Fir'awna qurratu 'aynin lee wa laka la taqtuloohu 'asa an yanfa'ana aw nattakhithahu waladan wa hum la yash'uroon,\" and the commentators note the plea, for the wife pleaded for the child, and the plea is the providence, the kindness that the Lord arranged, and the verse is the record of the plea, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the joy is the kindness, and the verse is the record of the plea, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the providence and the joy is the kindness, and the verse is the record of the plea, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the kindness of the Lord is the providence, and the one who is arranged is the one whose affair the Lord guides, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the plea of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: the wife pleaded and the child was kept, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the plea is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "10": "“And the heart of Moses’ mother ached so much that she almost gave away his identity, had We not reassured her heart in order for her to have faith ˹in Allah’s promise˺.”” records the ache, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the ache: or \"wa asbaha fu'adu ummi Moosa farighan in kadat la-tubdee bihi law la an rabatna 'ala qalbiha li-takoona mina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the ache, for the mother's heart ached, and the ache is the human, the grief of the mother, and the verse is the record of the ache, the grief that the reassurance calmed, and the commentators note the significance, for the reassurance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the ache, the grief of the mother. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ache is the human and the reassurance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the ache, the grief that the reassurance calmed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ache, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who grieves is the one whose grief the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the ache of the mother is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the remedy of the grief, and the believer who reads the ache is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reassurance of the Lord: the mother grieved and was calmed, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose grief the Lord removes, and the ache is the teaching that the trust is the remedy of the grief. The verse is the record of the ache, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "11": "“And she said to his sister, “Keep track of him!” So she watched him from a distance, while they were unaware.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"wa qalat li-ukhtihi qusseehi fa-basurat bihi 'an junubin wa hum la yash'uroon,\" and the commentators note the command, for the mother sent the sister, and the command is the providence, the arrangement of the watch, and the verse is the record of the command, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the watch is the providence, and the verse is the record of the command, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the providence and the watch is the arrangement, and the verse is the record of the command, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the providence of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who watches is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: the sister watched and the child was returned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the command is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "12": "“And We had caused him to refuse all wet-nurses at first, so his sister suggested, “Shall I direct you to a family who will bring him up for you and take good care of him?”” records the refusal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the refusal: or \"wa harramna 'alayhi al-maradi'a min qablu fa-qalat hal adullukum 'ala ahli baytin yakfuloonahu lakum wa hum lahu nasihoon,\" and the commentators note the refusal, for the child refused the wet-nurses, and the refusal is the providence, the arrangement of the return, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the providence that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal is the providence, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refusal is the providence and the return is the arrangement, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the providence that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refusal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the providence of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who is arranged is the one whose affair the Lord guides, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the refusal of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the refusal is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: the child refused and was returned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the refusal is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the refusal, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "13": "“This is how We returned him to his mother so that her heart would be put at ease, and not grieve, and that she would know that Allah’s promise is ˹always˺ true. But most people do not know.”” records the return, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the return: or \"fa-radadnahu ila ummihi kay taqarra 'aynuha wa la tahzana wa li-ta'lama anna wa'da Allahi haqqun wa lakinna aktharahum la ya'lamoon,\" and the commentators note the return, for the child was returned, and the return is the fulfillment, the promise of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the return, the fulfillment that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the return is the fulfillment, and the verse is the record of the return, the fulfillment of the promise. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the return is the fulfillment and the promise is the truth, and the verse is the record of the return, the fulfillment that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the return, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the promise of the Lord is the truth, and the one who trusts is the one whose promise the Lord fulfills, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the return of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the promise is the thing that the Lord fulfills, and the believer who reads the return is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the promise: the child was returned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose promise the Lord fulfills, and the return is the teaching that the promise is the thing that the Lord fulfills. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the trust that the fulfillment produces.",
  "14": "“And when he reached full strength and maturity, We gave him wisdom and knowledge. This is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the maturity, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the maturity: or \"wa lamma balagha ashuddahu wastawa ataynahu hukman wa 'ilman wa kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,\" and the commentators note the maturity, for Moses reached the strength and was granted, and the maturity is the growth, the reward of the good, and the verse is the record of the maturity, the reward that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the wisdom is the reward, and the verse is the record of the maturity, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the maturity is the growth and the reward is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the maturity, the reward that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the maturity, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own growth. The believer is taught that the wisdom is the reward, and the one who grows is the one whose wisdom the Lord grants, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord values, and the maturity of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the maturity is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the good: Moses was granted the wisdom, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord values, and the maturity is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the maturity, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "15": "“˹One day˺ he entered the city unnoticed by its people. There he found two men fighting: one of his own people, and the other of his enemies. The man from his people called to him for help against his foe. So Moses punched him, causing his death. Moses cried, “This is from Satan’s handiwork. He is certainly a sworn, misleading enemy.”” records the fight, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fight: or \"wa dakhala al-madeenata 'ala heeni ghaflatin min ahliha fa-wajada feeha rajulayni yaqtatilani hatha min shi'atihi wa hatha min 'aduwwihi fastaghathahu allathee min shi'atihi 'ala allathee min 'aduwwihi fa-wakazahu Moosa fa-qada 'alayhi qala hatha min 'amali ash-shaytani innahu 'aduwwun mudillun mubeen,\" and the commentators note the fight, for Moses struck the foe and killed him, and the fight is the slip, the killing of the man, and the verse is the record of the fight, the slip that the following verse confesses, and the commentators note the significance, for the killing is the slip, and the verse is the record of the fight, the slip of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fight is the slip and the killing is the error, and the verse is the record of the fight, the slip that the following verse confesses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fight, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the slip is the human, and the one who slips is the one whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord values, and the fight of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the slip is the thing that the repentance repairs, and the believer who reads the fight is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the slip: Moses slipped and repented, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the fight is the teaching that the slip is the thing that the repentance repairs. The verse is the record of the fight, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "16": "“He pleaded, “My Lord! I have definitely wronged my soul, so forgive me.” So He forgave him, ˹for˺ He is indeed the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the plea: or \"qala rabbi innee zalamtu nafsee faghfir lee fa-ghafara lahu innahu huwa al-ghafooru ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the plea, for Moses confessed and sought the forgiveness, and the plea is the repentance, the confession of the wrong, and the verse is the record of the plea, the repentance that the following verse pledges, and the commentators note the significance, for the forgiveness is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the plea, the repentance of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the repentance and the forgiveness is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the plea, the repentance that the following verse pledges.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the confession of the wrong is the repentance, and the one who confesses is the one whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the believer who repents is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the plea of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the repentance is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the repentance of the wrong: Moses confessed and was forgiven, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the plea is the teaching that the repentance is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the hope that the forgiveness carries.",
  "17": "“Moses pledged, “My Lord! For all Your favours upon me, I will never side with the wicked.”” records the pledge, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the pledge: or \"qala rabbi bima an'amta 'alayya fa-lan akoona zaheeran lil-mujrimeen,\" and the commentators note the pledge, for Moses pledged the loyalty, and the pledge is the resolve, the declaration of the side, and the verse is the record of the pledge, the resolve that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the pledge is the resolve, and the verse is the record of the pledge, the resolve of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pledge is the resolve and the loyalty is the side, and the verse is the record of the pledge, the resolve that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own resolve. The believer is taught that the pledge of the loyalty is the resolve, and the one who pledges is the one whose resolve the Lord values, and the believer who sides with the truth is the believer whose resolve the Lord accepts, and the pledge of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the resolve is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the pledge is the believer whose resolve the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the resolve of the loyalty: Moses pledged the side, and the believer who sides with the truth is the believer whose resolve the Lord values, and the pledge is the teaching that the resolve is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the pledge, and the teaching of the resolve that the loyalty requires.",
  "18": "“And so Moses became fearful, watching out in the city, when suddenly the one who sought his help the day before cried out to him again for help. Moses rebuked him, “Indeed, you are clearly a trouble-maker.”” records the fear, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fear: or \"fa-asbaha fi al-madeenati kha'ifan yataraqqabu fa-itha allathee istansarahu bil-amsi yastasrikhuhu qala lahu Moosa innaka la-ghawiyyun mubeen,\" and the commentators note the fear, for Moses became fearful, and the fear is the state, the caution of the fugitive, and the verse is the record of the fear, the caution that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear is the state, and the verse is the record of the fear, the caution of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fear is the state and the caution is the watch, and the verse is the record of the fear, the caution that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the fear of the trial is the human, and the one who fears is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the fear of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the human, and the believer who reads the fear is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: Moses feared and was guided, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the fear is the teaching that the trust is the remedy. The verse is the record of the fear, and the teaching of the trust that the caution requires.",
  "19": "“Then when Moses was about to lay his hands on their foe, the enemy said, “O Moses! Do you intend to kill me as you killed a man yesterday? You only want to be a tyrant in the land. You do not intend to make peace!”” records the exposure, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the exposure: or \"fa-lamma an arada an yabtisha billathee huwa 'aduwwun lahuma qala ya Moosa a-tureedu an taqtulanee kama qatalta nafsan bil-amsi in tureedu illa an takoona jabbaran fi al-ardi wa ma tureedu an takoona mina al-musliheen,\" and the commentators note the exposure, for the enemy exposed the killing, and the exposure is the turning, the revelation of the secret, and the verse is the record of the exposure, the turning that the following verse resolves, and the commentators note the significance, for the exposure is the turning, and the verse is the record of the exposure, the turning of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exposure is the turning and the revelation is the secret, and the verse is the record of the exposure, the turning that the following verse resolves.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exposure, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the exposure of the secret is the turning, and the one who is exposed is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the exposure of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the affair is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the exposure is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: Moses was exposed and fled, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the exposure is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the exposure, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "20": "“And there came a man, rushing from the farthest end of the city. He said, “O Moses! The chiefs are actually conspiring against you to put you to death, so leave ˹the city˺. I really advise you ˹to do so˺.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The verse records the warning: or \"wa ja'a rajulun min aqsa al-madeenati yas'a qala ya Moosa inna al-mala'a ya'tamiroona bika li-yaqtulooka fakhruj innee laka mina an-nasiheen,\" and the commentators note the warning, for the man warned Moses, and the warning is the providence, the rescue of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the warning, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the providence, and the verse is the record of the warning, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the providence and the rescue is the advice, and the verse is the record of the warning, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the warning of the Lord is the providence, and the one who is warned is the one whose rescue the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the warning of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: Moses was warned and fled, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose rescue the Lord arranges, and the warning is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "21": "“So Moses left the city in a state of fear and caution, praying, “My Lord! Deliver me from the wrongdoing people.”” records the flight, and the commentators note the two parts of the flight. The verse records the flight: or \"fa-kharaja minha kha'ifan yataraqqabu qala rabbi najjinee mina al-qawmi az-zalimeen,\" and the commentators note the flight, for Moses fled and prayed, and the flight is the trust, the departure of the fugitive, and the verse is the record of the flight, the trust that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the flight is the trust, and the verse is the record of the flight, the trust of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the flight is the trust and the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the flight, the trust that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the flight, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the flight of the believer is the trust, and the one who flees is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who prays in the flight is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the flight of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the flight is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the fugitive: Moses fled and prayed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the flight is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the flight, and the teaching of the trust that the departure requires.",
  "22": "“And as he made his way towards Midian, he said, “I trust my Lord will guide me to the right way.”” records the hope, and the commentators note the two parts of the hope. The verse records the hope: or \"wa lamma tawajjaha tilqa'a Madyana qala 'asa rabbee an yahdiyanee sawa'a as-sabeel,\" and the commentators note the hope, for Moses hoped the guidance, and the hope is the trust, the reliance of the fugitive, and the verse is the record of the hope, the trust that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the hope is the trust, and the verse is the record of the hope, the trust of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hope is the trust and the guidance is the request, and the verse is the record of the hope, the trust that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the hope of the guidance is the trust, and the one who hopes is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the hope of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the hope is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the fugitive: Moses hoped and was guided, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the hope is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the hope, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance requires.",
  "23": "“When he arrived at the well of Midian, he found a group of people watering ˹their herds˺. Apart from them, he noticed two women holding back ˹their herd˺. He asked ˹them˺, “What is the problem?” They replied, “We cannot water ˹our animals˺ until the ˹other˺ shepherds are done, for our father is a very old man.”” records the arrival, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the arrival: or \"wa lamma warada ma'a Madyana wajada 'alayhi ummatan mina an-nasi yasqoona wa wajada min doonihimu imra'atayni tathoodani qala ma khatbukuma qalata la nasqee hatta yusdira ar-ri'a'u wa aboona shaykhun kabeer,\" and the commentators note the arrival, for Moses arrived at the well and helped the women, and the arrival is the providence, the beginning of the settlement, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the providence that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the help is the kindness, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the providence of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the arrival is the providence and the help is the kindness, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the providence that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the arrival, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the providence of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who helps is the one whose kindness the Lord values, and the believer who serves the weak is the believer whose kindness the Lord accepts, and the arrival of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges, and the believer who reads the arrival is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the Lord: Moses helped the women, and the believer who serves the weak is the believer whose kindness the Lord values, and the arrival is the teaching that the providence is the thing that the Lord arranges. The verse is the record of the arrival, and the teaching of the trust that the providence produces.",
  "24": "“So he watered ˹their herd˺ for them, then withdrew to the shade and prayed, “My Lord! I am truly in ˹desperate˺ need of whatever provision You may have in store for me.”” records the watering and the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the watering: or \"fa-saqa lahuma thumma tawalla ila az-zilli fa-qala rabbi innee lima anzalta ilayya min khayrin faqeer,\" and the commentators note the watering, for Moses watered and prayed, and the watering is the kindness, the help of the stranger, and the prayer is the reliance, the seeking of the provision, and the verse is the record of the watering, the kindness and the reliance that the following verse rewards, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the watering, the kindness of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the watering is the kindness and the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the watering, the kindness that the following verse rewards.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the watering, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own kindness. The believer is taught that the help of the weak is the kindness, and the one who helps is the one whose kindness the Lord values, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the watering of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the kindness and the reliance are the things that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the watering is the believer whose kindness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the kindness of the help: Moses watered and prayed, and the believer who helps the weak is the believer whose kindness the Lord values, and the watering is the teaching that the kindness and the reliance are the things that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the watering, and the teaching of the kindness that the help requires.",
  "25": "“Then one of the two women came to him, walking bashfully. She said, “My father is inviting you so he may reward you for watering ˹our animals˺ for us.” When Moses came to him and told him his whole story, the old man said, “Have no fear! You are ˹now˺ safe from the wrongdoing people.”” records the invitation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the invitation: or \"fa-ja'athu ihdahuma tamshee 'ala istihya'in qalat inna abee yad'ooka li-yajziyaka ajra ma saqayta lana fa-lamma ja'ahu wa qassa 'alayhi al-qasasa qala la takhaf najawta mina al-qawmi az-zalimeen,\" and the commentators note the invitation, for the woman invited Moses, and the invitation is the reward, the beginning of the settlement, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the reward that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the safety is the promise, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the reward of the kindness. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the invitation is the reward and the safety is the promise, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the reward that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the invitation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the reward of the kindness is the providence, and the one who helps is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the invitation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the kindness is the thing that the Lord rewards, and the believer who reads the invitation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the kindness: Moses was invited and settled, and the believer who helps the weak is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the invitation is the teaching that the kindness is the thing that the Lord rewards. The verse is the record of the invitation, and the teaching of the trust that the reward produces.",
  "26": "“One of the two daughters suggested, “O my dear father! Hire him. The best man for employment is definitely the strong and trustworthy ˹one˺.”” records the suggestion, and the commentators note the two parts of the suggestion. The verse records the suggestion: or \"qalat ihdahuma ya abati ista'jirhu inna khayra mani ista'jarta al-qawiyyu al-ameen,\" and the commentators note the suggestion, for the daughter suggested the hire, and the suggestion is the wisdom, the recognition of the qualities, and the verse is the record of the suggestion, the wisdom that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the two qualities are the strength and the trust, and the verse is the record of the suggestion, the wisdom of the daughter. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the suggestion is the wisdom and the two qualities are the strength and the trust, and the verse is the record of the suggestion, the wisdom that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the suggestion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the recognition of the qualities is the justice, and the one who recognizes is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who weighs the trust is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the suggestion of the daughter is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must weigh, and the believer who reads the suggestion is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the trust: the daughter recognized the qualities, and the believer who weighs the trust is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the suggestion is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must weigh. The verse is the record of the suggestion, and the teaching of the justice that the trust requires.",
  "27": "“The old man proposed, “I wish to marry one of these two daughters of mine to you, provided that you stay in my service for eight years. If you complete ten, it will be ˹a favour˺ from you, but I do not wish to make it difficult for you. Allah willing, you will find me an agreeable man.”” records the proposal, and the commentators note the two parts of the proposal. The verse records the proposal: or \"qala innee ureedu an unkihaka ihda ibnatayya hatayni 'ala an ta'juranee thamaniya hijajin fa-in atmamta 'ashran fa-min 'indika wa ma ureedu an ashuqqa 'alayka satajidunee in sha'a Allahu mina as-saliheen,\" and the commentators note the proposal, for the old man proposed the marriage, and the proposal is the covenant, the arrangement of the settlement, and the verse is the record of the proposal, the covenant that the following verse accepts, and the commentators note the significance, for the marriage is the settlement, and the verse is the record of the proposal, the covenant of the old man. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the proposal is the covenant and the marriage is the settlement, and the verse is the record of the proposal, the covenant that the following verse accepts.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the proposal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the covenant is the trust, and the one who proposes is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who honors the word is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the proposal of the old man is the teaching, the declaration that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the proposal is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the covenant: the old man proposed and Moses accepted, and the believer who honors the word is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the proposal is the teaching that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the proposal, and the teaching of the honesty that the covenant requires.",
  "28": "“Moses responded, “˹Then˺ it is ˹settled˺ between you and I. Whichever term I fulfill, there will be no ˹further˺ obligation on me. And Allah is a Witness to what we say.”” records the acceptance, and the commentators note the two parts of the acceptance. The verse records the acceptance: or \"qala thalika baynee wa baynaka ayyama al-ajalayni qadaytu fa-la 'udwana 'alayya wallahu 'ala ma naqoolu wakeel,\" and the commentators note the acceptance, for Moses accepted the covenant, and the acceptance is the settlement, the agreement of the two, and the verse is the record of the acceptance, the settlement that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the witness is the trust, and the verse is the record of the acceptance, the settlement of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the acceptance is the settlement and the witness is the trust, and the verse is the record of the acceptance, the settlement that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the acceptance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the honoring of the covenant is the trust, and the one who accepts is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who keeps the word is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the acceptance of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the acceptance is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the covenant: Moses accepted and kept, and the believer who honors the word is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the acceptance is the teaching that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the acceptance, and the teaching of the honesty that the covenant requires.",
  "29": "“When Moses had completed the term and was travelling with his family, he spotted a fire on the side of Mount Ṭûr. He said to his family, “Stay here, ˹for˺ I have spotted a fire. Perhaps from there I can bring you some directions or a torch from the fire so you may warm yourselves.”” records the departure, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the departure: or \"fa-lamma qada Moosa al-ajala wa sara bi-ahlihi anasa min janibi at-toori naran qala li-ahlihi umkuthoo innee anastu naran la'allee ateekum minha bi-khabarin aw jathwatin mina an-nari la'allakum tastaloon,\" and the commentators note the departure, for Moses completed the term and saw the fire, and the departure is the seeking, the hope of the directions, and the verse is the record of the departure, the seeking that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the fire is the sign, and the verse is the record of the departure, the seeking of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the departure is the seeking and the fire is the sign, and the verse is the record of the departure, the seeking that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the departure, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own striving. The believer is taught that the seeking of the means is the duty, and the one who seeks is the one whose striving the Lord values, and the believer who travels for the provision is the believer whose striving the Lord accepts, and the departure of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the seeking is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the departure is the believer whose striving the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the means: Moses sought the torch and found the call, and the believer who seeks the means is the believer whose striving the Lord values, and the departure is the teaching that the seeking is the path of the guidance. The verse is the record of the departure, and the teaching of the striving that the seeking requires.",
  "30": "“But when he came to it, he was called from the bush in the sacred ground to the right side of the valley: “O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Lord of all worlds.”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the call. The verse records the call: or \"fa-lamma ataha noodiya min shati'i al-wadi al-aymani fi al-buq'ati al-mubarakati mina ash-shajarati an ya Moosa innee ana Allahu rabbu al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the call, for the Lord called Moses from the bush, and the call is the revelation, the beginning of the mission, and the verse is the record of the call, the revelation that the following verse commands, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the revelation, and the verse is the record of the call, the revelation of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the revelation and the bush is the place, and the verse is the record of the call, the revelation that the following verse commands.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reverence. The believer is taught that the call of the Lord is the revelation, and the one who is called is the one whose reverence the Lord values, and the believer who hears the call is the believer whose reverence the Lord accepts, and the call to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the revelation is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose reverence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the revelation of the Lord: Moses was called and sent, and the believer who hears the call is the believer whose reverence the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the revelation is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the reverence that the revelation requires.",
  "31": "“Now, throw down your staff!” But when he saw it slithering like a snake, he ran away without looking back. ˹Allah reassured him,˺ “O Moses! Draw near, and have no fear. You are perfectly secure.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"wa an alqi 'asaka fa-lamma ra'aha tahtazzu ka-annaha jannun walla mudbiran wa lam yu'aqqib ya Moosa aqbil wa la takhaf innaka mina al-amineen,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the throwing and reassured the fear, and the command is the sign, the demonstration of the power, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign and the reassurance that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the transformation is the sign, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the sign and the reassurance is the comfort, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who fears is the one whose fear the Lord removes, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the presence is the safety, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the presence of the Lord: Moses feared and was reassured, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose fear the Lord removes, and the command is the teaching that the presence is the protection. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the trust that the presence produces.",
  "32": "“Now put your hand through ˹the opening of˺ your collar, it will come out ˹shining˺ white, unblemished. And cross your arms tightly to calm your fears. These are two proofs from your Lord to Pharaoh and his chiefs. They have truly been a rebellious people.”” records the second sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"usluk yadaka fee jaybika takhruj bayda'a min ghayri soo'in wadmum ilayka janahaka mina ar-rahbi fa-thanika burhanani min rabbika ila Fir'awna wa mala'ihi innahum kanoo qawman fasiqeen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the hand shone white, and the sign is the proof, the second of the signs, and the verse is the record of the sign, the proof that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the two proofs are the signs, and the verse is the record of the sign, the proof of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the proof and the two are the signs, and the verse is the record of the sign, the proof that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the proofs, and the one who receives is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the sign of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the hand shone white, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the light is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "33": "“Moses appealed, “My Lord! I have indeed killed a man from them, so I fear they may kill me.”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: or \"qala rabbi innee qataltu minhum nafsan fa-akhafu an yaqtuloon,\" and the commentators note the plea, for Moses feared the charge, and the plea is the human, the confession of the fear, and the verse is the record of the plea, the fear that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear is the human, and the verse is the record of the plea, the fear of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the fear and the charge is the killing, and the verse is the record of the plea, the fear that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the fear of the trial is the human, and the one who fears is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the plea of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the human, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: Moses feared and was reassured, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the plea is the teaching that the trust is the remedy. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "34": "“And my brother Aaron is more eloquent than I, so send him with me as a helper to support what I say, for I truly fear they may reject me.”” completes the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the plea: or \"wa akhee Haroonu huwa afsahu minnee lisanan fa-arsilhu ma'iya rid'an yusaddiqunee innee akhafu an yukaththiboon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Moses asked the helper, and the completion is the plea, the request of the support, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plea that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the request is the plea, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plea of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the plea and the request is the helper, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plea that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the request of the helper is the reliance, and the one who asks is the one whose help the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the completion of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the weak: Moses asked the helper, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose help the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the request requires.",
  "35": "“Allah responded, “We will assist you with your brother and grant you both authority, so they cannot harm you. With Our signs, you and those who follow you will ˹certainly˺ prevail.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: or \"qala sa-nashuddu 'adudaka bi-akheeka wa naj'alu lakuma sultanan fa-la yasiloona ilaykuma bi-ayatina antuma wa mani ittaba'akuma al-ghaliboon,\" and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord granted the helper and the authority, and the answer is the reassurance, the promise of the victory, and the verse is the record of the answer, the reassurance that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the victory is the promise, and the verse is the record of the answer, the reassurance of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the reassurance and the victory is the promise, and the verse is the record of the answer, the reassurance that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the promise of the Lord is the victory, and the one who trusts is the one whose victory the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the answer to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the victory is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the promise of the victory: Moses was granted and prevailed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose victory the Lord grants, and the answer is the teaching that the victory is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the trust that the promise produces.",
  "36": "“But when Moses came to them with Our clear signs, they said ˹arrogantly˺, “This is nothing but conjured magic ˹tricks˺. We have never heard of this in ˹the history of˺ our forefathers.”” records the rejection, and the commentators note the two parts of the rejection. The verse records the rejection: or \"fa-lamma ja'ahum Moosa bi-ayatina bayyinatin qaloo ma hatha illa sihrun muftaran wa ma sami'na bihatha fee aba'ina al-awwaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people called the signs the magic, and the rejection is the label, the charge of the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the label and the magic is the charge, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the magic is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the rejection of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Moses was labelled and prevailed, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "37": "“Moses responded, “My Lord knows best who has come with ˹true˺ guidance from Him and will fare best in the end. Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"wa qala Moosa rabbee a'lamu biman ja'a bil-huda min 'indihi wa man takoonu lahu 'aqibatu ad-dari innahu la yuflihu az-zalimoon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Moses attributed the knowledge to the Lord, and the reply is the reliance, the declaration of the trust, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the attribution is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the reliance and the attribution is the trust, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the attribution of the knowledge is the reliance, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the prophet: Moses attributed the knowledge, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the trust that the reliance requires.",
  "38": "“Pharaoh declared, “O chiefs! I know of no other god for you but myself. So bake bricks out of clay for me, O Hamân, and build a high tower so I may look at the God of Moses, although I am sure he is a liar.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the declaration. The verse records the declaration: or \"wa qala Fir'awnu ya ayyuha al-mala'u ma 'alimtu lakum min ilahin ghayree fa-awqid lee ya Hamanu 'ala at-teeni faj'al lee sarhan la'allee attali'u ila ilahi Moosa wa innee la-azunnuhu mina al-kathibeen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Pharaoh claimed the divinity and ordered the tower, and the declaration is the arrogance, the claim of the tyrant, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the arrogance that the following verse condemns, and the commentators note the significance, for the claim is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the arrogance of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the arrogance and the claim is the divinity, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the arrogance that the following verse condemns.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the claim of the divinity is the arrogance, and the one who claims is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the declaration of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the arrogance: Pharaoh claimed and drowned, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the humility that the arrogance requires.",
  "39": "“And so he and his soldiers behaved arrogantly in the land with no right, thinking they would never be returned to Us.”” records the arrogance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the arrogance: or \"wastakbara huwa wa junooduhu fi al-ardi bi-ghayri al-haqqi wa zannoo annahum ilayna la yurja'oon,\" and the commentators note the arrogance, for Pharaoh and the soldiers behaved arrogantly, and the arrogance is the sin, the denial of the return, and the verse is the record of the arrogance, the sin that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the arrogance is the sin, and the verse is the record of the arrogance, the sin of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the arrogance is the sin and the denial is the return, and the verse is the record of the arrogance, the sin that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the arrogance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the arrogance is the sin, and the one who is arrogant is the one whose sin the Lord judges, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the arrogance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the arrogance is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the arrogance: Pharaoh was arrogant and drowned, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the arrogance is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the arrogance, and the teaching of the humility that the arrogance requires.",
  "40": "“So We seized him and his soldiers, casting them into the sea. See then what was the end of the wrongdoers!”” records the drowning, and the commentators note the two parts of the drowning. The verse records the drowning: or \"fa-akhathnahu wa junoodahu fa-nabathnahum fi al-yammi fanzur kayfa kana 'aqibatu az-zalimeen,\" and the commentators note the drowning, for the Lord seized Pharaoh and drowned him, and the drowning is the judgment, the end of the tyrant, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the drowning is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the drowning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the drowning of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the drowning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the tyrant drowned and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the drowning, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "41": "“We made them leaders inviting ˹others˺ to the Fire. And on the Day of Judgment they will not be helped.”” records the leadership, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the leadership: or \"wa ja'alnahum a'immatan yad'oona ila an-nari wa yawma al-qiyamati la yunsaroon,\" and the commentators note the leadership, for the Lord made them leaders to the Fire, and the leadership is the judgment, the description of the misguidance, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the leadership is the misguidance, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the leadership is the misguidance and the judgment is the end, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the leadership, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own responsibility. The believer is taught that the leadership is the trust, and the one who leads is the one whose leadership the Lord weighs, and the believer who leads to the truth is the believer whose leadership the Lord values, and the leadership of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the misguidance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the leadership is the believer whose responsibility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the leadership: Pharaoh led to the Fire, and the believer who leads to the truth is the believer whose leadership the Lord values, and the leadership is the teaching that the misguidance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the leadership, and the teaching of the responsibility that the leadership requires.",
  "42": "“We caused a curse to follow them in this world. And on the Day of Judgment they will be among the outcasts.”” records the curse, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the curse: or \"wa atba'nahum fee hathihi ad-dunya la'natan wa yawma al-qiyamati hum mina al-maqbooheen,\" and the commentators note the curse, for the Lord caused the curse to follow them, and the curse is the judgment, the distance of the mercy, and the verse is the record of the curse, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the curse is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the curse, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the curse is the judgment and the outcast is the end, and the verse is the record of the curse, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the curse, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the curse of the Lord is the judgment, and the one who is cursed is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the curse of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the curse is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the curse followed the tyrants, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the curse is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the curse, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "43": "“Indeed, We gave Moses the Scripture—after destroying earlier nations —as an insight for the people, a guide, and mercy so perhaps they would be mindful.”” records the Scripture, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the Scripture: or \"wa laqad atayna Moosa al-kitaba min ba'di ma ahlakna al-quroona al-oola basa'ira lin-nasi wa hudan wa rahmatan la'allahum yatathakkaroon,\" and the commentators note the Scripture, for the Lord gave Moses the Book, and the Scripture is the gift, the guide of the people, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the Book is the gift, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Scripture is the gift and the guide is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the Scripture, the gift that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the Scripture, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own guidance. The believer is taught that the Scripture of the Lord is the guide, and the one who is given is the one whose guidance the Lord values, and the believer who follows the Book is the believer whose guidance the Lord accepts, and the Scripture of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the guide is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the Scripture is the believer whose guidance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the Scripture: Moses was given the Book, and the believer who follows the guide is the believer whose guidance the Lord values, and the Scripture is the teaching that the guide is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the Scripture, and the teaching of the guidance that the Book grants.",
  "44": "“You were not there, O Prophet, on the western side of the mountain when We entrusted the Commandments to Moses, nor were you present in his time.” The argument turns here from storytelling to proof. The Quran has just recounted events from the life of Moses in vivid detail. Now it points out something obvious but devastating: the Prophet Muhammad was not there. He never stood on the western side of the mountain where Moses received the Commandments. He lived centuries later, in a different place, among a different people. Yet he relates these events with complete accuracy.\n\nThis is one of the Quran’s recurring challenges to its hearers. How could an unlettered man in Mecca describe the meeting between God and Moses at the mountain unless the information came directly from God? The detail itself, “the western side of the mountain,” is the kind of specific touch that a fabricator would be unlikely to invent and unable to verify. It is offered as evidence that the source of the story is revelation, not legend or borrowed learning.\n\nThe verse also honours Moses by recalling the moment he was entrusted with the Scripture. That moment on the mountain was the turning point of his mission, the place where the law was given and the covenant sealed. By reminding the listener of it, the Quran places Muhammad’s revelation in the same line as Moses’. Both received their message directly from the same Lord, on sacred ground.\n\nThere is a gentle reassurance in this for the Prophet himself. He was being accused by the powerful of Mecca of inventing tales, of copying old stories. This verse tells him that the very stories they mocked were his proof, not his weakness. A person who could not have known these things, yet knew them, must have been taught by the One who was present at every moment of history. The same argument still speaks to anyone who reads the Quran carefully today and finds in its detail a sign of a source beyond human invention.",
  "45": "“But We later raised several generations, and the ages took their toll on them.” Long centuries passed between Moses and Muhammad. Empires rose and fell, languages shifted, and the memory of those events faded into the dust of time. The verse paints this passage of years as something that wore people down, “the ages took their toll on them.” Whole generations came and went, and with them the living chain of eyewitnesses to Moses’ story disappeared. The point is to underline the size of the gap. Muhammad could not have learned these details from anyone alive, because no one alive had been there.\n\n“Nor were you living among the people of Midian, rehearsing Our revelations with them.” Midian was the land where Moses lived for years after fleeing Egypt, the place where he married, tended sheep, and learned from the prophet Shu‘ayb. The verse names it specifically. Even there, in the very region connected to Moses’ life, the Prophet of Islam had never lived or sat studying these old accounts. He was a merchant of Mecca, not a scholar of Midian.\n\n“But it is We Who have sent this revelation to you.” After two statements of where he was not, comes the single decisive statement of where his knowledge comes from. He was not at the mountain, not among the people of Midian, not present in any of those centuries; therefore the only possible source of his accurate knowledge is God Himself. The structure of the verse is itself a kind of proof, narrowing the options down until only revelation remains.\n\nThis passage should be read together with the verse before it. Both make the same argument from absence: the Prophet could not have known these things naturally, so his knowing them is itself a sign. For the believer, this builds certainty; for the honest sceptic, it is an invitation to think. The Quran is not afraid to rest its case on history, because it knows that the more carefully a person examines these details, the clearer the hand of revelation becomes.",
  "46": "“And you were not at the side of Mount Ṭûr when We called out to Moses.” The argument from the previous verses continues, now with even more specificity. Mount Ṭûr, the mountain where God spoke to Moses, is named directly. The Prophet of Islam never stood in its shadow and never heard that voice with his own ears. Yet he speaks of it as one who knows. The cumulative effect of these verses of absence, each one stressing that he was not present, is to make the source of his knowledge undeniable.\n\n“But you have been sent as a mercy from your Lord to warn a people to whom no warner has come before you, so perhaps they may be mindful.” After the proof comes the purpose. Muhammad was not present at the old revelations because he himself was to be a revelation, a messenger in his own right. His mission is described first as “a mercy.” This is remarkable. He is sent to warn, but the warning itself is framed as mercy, because a genuine warning rescues people from a danger they cannot see. A doctor who tells a patient about a hidden illness is being kind, not cruel, even if the news is frightening.\n\nHe is sent “to warn a people to whom no warner has come before you.” The Arabs of Mecca had not received a messenger in the line of the great prophets. They had been left, in a sense, without a scripture of their own. Now one had arrived, and the arrival was an act of divine favour, not a burden. They were being included in the same chain of guidance that had once reached Moses and the others.\n\n“So perhaps they may be mindful” closes the verse with the same goal we saw when the Scripture was given to Moses. Every messenger, every revelation, every warning ultimately aims at the same thing: that people might wake up, become aware of God, and live with that awareness shaping their choices. The word “perhaps” keeps human freedom intact. The mercy is offered; the response is left to each heart.",
  "47": "“Also so they would not say, if struck by an affliction for what their hands have done: Our Lord! If only You had sent us a messenger, we would have followed Your revelations and become believers.” This verse uncovers one of the deepest reasons God sends messengers: to leave people without excuse. Human beings are skilled at shifting blame. When suffering finally catches up with them, the temptation is to protest that they were never warned, that if only a guide had come they would surely have done the right thing.\n\nThe Quran refuses to let that complaint stand unchallenged. By sending a clear messenger before any punishment, God removes the most common defence a guilty soul can offer on the Day of Judgment. The verse even puts the exact words of the imagined complaint into their mouths, “If only You had sent us a messenger,” because it knows the human heart so well. It anticipates the excuse and answers it in advance.\n\nNotice the careful phrase “for what their hands have done.” Affliction in this life is often tied to people’s own wrongdoing. When corruption, injustice, or heedlessness finally produce their bitter fruit, the suffering is not random cruelty; it is the harvest of choices that were freely made. The verse does not say that every hardship is a punishment, because the Quran elsewhere is clear that trials also purify and elevate the believers. Here the focus is specifically on the affliction that follows sin.\n\nThere is a profound justice in this arrangement. God does not punish without first making the truth plain. A warning is sent, the path is shown, the consequences are explained, and time is given to turn back. Only after all of that does accountability arrive. This protects God’s fairness from any accusation, and it places the responsibility for the outcome squarely where it belongs, on the human beings who were given the message and chose how to respond to it. The sending of the Prophet was therefore both a mercy and a safeguard, an act of grace that closed the door on the plea of ignorance forever.",
  "48": "“But when the truth came to them from Us, they said, If only he was given the like of what Moses had been given.” The people of Mecca had spent years demanding a sign. Yet when the truth actually arrived, they did not embrace it; they simply moved the goalposts. Now they complained that Muhammad had not been given the same kind of spectacular miracles as Moses. This is a pattern the Quran exposes again and again: the demand for a sign is often a mask, not a genuine search for truth. When one proof is offered, the doubter simply asks for another.\n\nThe verse immediately turns their objection back on them: “Did they not deny what had been given to Moses earlier?” This is the sting of the argument. The very Scripture they now claimed to admire, the Torah given to Moses, was itself rejected by people in its own time. So their demand was dishonest. Even if Moses-style signs were shown to them, they would find a reason to refuse those too. The problem was never the absence of proof; it was the stubbornness of the heart that had already decided not to believe.\n\n“They claimed, Both Scriptures are works of magic, supporting each other!” Faced with two revelations that agree with one another, the doubters reached for a convenient accusation. They labelled both the Torah and the Quran as magic, and explained away their agreement by saying the two “supported each other.” It is a striking example of how a person determined not to believe will twist even the strongest evidence, the harmony between two scriptures, into a reason for doubt. Agreement that should have confirmed the truth was reinterpreted as collusion.\n\n“We truly deny both,” they added. The mask comes off. At bottom this was never a careful weighing of evidence. It was a simple, stubborn refusal. The verse trains the reader to recognise this shape of argument, the endless shifting of demands and the readiness to slander whatever proof appears, and to understand that such responses come from a will that has already closed itself, not from a mind that is genuinely seeking the truth.",
  "49": "“Say, O Prophet, Bring then a scripture from Allah which is a better guide than these two so I may follow it, if your claim is true.” Having exposed the dishonesty of their objections, the Quran issues a direct challenge. If the doubters truly believed that the Torah and the Quran were merely human inventions, and that they themselves could recognise real guidance when they saw it, then let them produce something better. Let them bring even one scripture from God that offers clearer guidance than these two.\n\nThe challenge is deliberately impossible to meet, and it is framed with extraordinary confidence. The Prophet is told to say that if such a scripture exists, he himself would follow it. A false prophet would never dare invite people to bring a competing revelation and promise to abandon his own mission for it. Only someone certain of the divine origin of his message could speak this way. The invitation itself becomes a proof.\n\n“These two” refers to the Torah and the Quran together. The verse places them side by side as two authentic revelations from the same source. It honours the Scripture of Moses even while defending the Quran, because both come from the same Lord and both lead in the same direction. This is an important principle: the Quran does not regard the earlier scriptures, in their original revealed form, as rivals to be dismissed. It treats them as part of one continuous guidance.\n\nThis challenge echoes a famous one found elsewhere in the Quran, where the doubters are invited to produce even a single chapter like it if they doubt its divine origin, and are told that they will never be able to do so, even if they helped one another. Here the form of the challenge is gentler but the logic is the same. When human beings cannot match something, yet refuse to accept it, their failure to respond becomes the clearest answer of all. The silence of the critics speaks louder than any argument.",
  "50": "“So if they fail to respond to you, then know that they only follow their desires.” The previous verse issued a challenge they could not meet. This verse draws the conclusion from their failure. When a person has been offered clear guidance and a fair test, yet still refuses, the real reason is usually not intellectual. It is that they are being pulled by something inside themselves. The Arabic word often used for this is “hawa,” a driver of the lower self, and it is one of the most dangerous forces the Quran warns about, because it disguises itself as reason while really being appetite and habit.\n\n“And who could be more astray than those who follow their desires with no guidance from Allah?” This is a searching question, and its answer is obvious: no one. There is no path more lost than the one where a person’s own wants become the compass. The tragedy of following desire is that it feels like freedom. The person thinks they are choosing for themselves, when in fact they are being led by cravings, fears, social pressure, and old habits. Without a light from outside themselves, from God, they have no fixed point by which to tell true from false.\n\nNotice the precision of the phrase “with no guidance from Allah.” Desire itself is not the whole problem; the problem is following it in the absence of divine guidance. Guidance acts as a boundary and a light. It lets a person examine their wants honestly, keep the good ones, and discipline the harmful ones. Strip that guidance away, and desire simply expands until it rules everything.\n\n“Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.” The verse ends with a sober principle. Divine guidance is not given to everyone indiscriminately. Those who insist on wrongdoing, who close their ears and choose oppression or arrogance, are left to the path they have chosen. Guidance is a gift that responds to sincerity. This is not cruelty; it is the natural consequence of a heart that has repeatedly pushed the light away. The warning is gentle but clear: the desire to be guided must itself be genuine, because God guides those who turn toward Him in honesty.",
  "51": "“Indeed, We have steadily delivered the Word of Allah to the people so they may be mindful.” After the stern warnings of the previous verses, this one returns to the patience of divine teaching. The Quran was not simply dropped on its audience all at once. It was delivered “steadily,” piece by piece, over the years of the Prophet’s mission, in a rhythm that matched the events and needs of the community. A verse of comfort came in grief, a verse of law came when the situation required it, a verse of warning came when hearts had grown heedless. This careful pacing was itself a mercy.\n\nThe phrase “steadily delivered” carries the sense of something being brought close and made to arrive at its proper time. The Quran describes itself elsewhere as a Book whose parts reinforce one another, repeated and arranged so that the message can sink in. Repetition in the Quran is not a flaw; it is a deliberate teaching method. The same truth returns in new wording and new context so that it can reach different hearts and find entrance through different doors.\n\nThe goal is named plainly: “so they may be mindful.” Once again the destination of all revelation is “taqwa,” that alert, living awareness of God. Every verse, whether it tells a story, lays down a law, or paints a scene of the Hereafter, is aimed at the same target, waking the human heart up to the reality of its Lord and the weight of its own choices.\n\nThere is great encouragement hidden in this verse. The fact that the Word was delivered steadily means that God understands how human beings learn. Hearts do not transform overnight, and revelation does not demand that they do. It is patient with our slowness. It offers the light again and again, in varied forms, giving every person the chance to absorb it at the pace they need. For anyone who feels their faith is weak or their understanding is slow, this verse is a comfort: the same steady Word that was delivered to the first community is still being delivered, every time it is read with an open heart, to do its quiet work of making us mindful.",
  "52": "“As for those faithful to whom We had given the Scripture before this Quran, they do believe in it.” The Quran now turns to a beautiful and important group, the sincere believers from among the People of the Book. These were Jews and Christians who had held faithfully to the scriptures given to them and who, when they heard the Quran, recognised it instantly. Their faith was not divided; the same light that had come to them in their own Book now shone in this new revelation, and they embraced it without hesitation.\n\nThe verse honours them with the word “faithful.” They had been true to the covenant they already had. Because they had kept the earlier light alive in their hearts, they were able to recognise the same light when it returned in a new form. This is one of the great principles of spiritual life: the person who is honest with the truth they already possess will be given more. God does not ask people to abandon sincerity; He rewards it by leading them forward into fuller guidance.\n\nThis verse also corrects a common misunderstanding. The Quran does not treat all People of the Book as a single block. It distinguishes sharply between those who corrupted or rejected the truth and those who preserved and lived by it. The faithful among them are praised in the warmest terms, while the corrupters are warned. Fairness, not blanket judgment, is the Quranic way.\n\nRead against the previous verses, this is a striking contrast. The powerful of Mecca, who had been given no scripture of their own, refused the clear truth when it came and invented endless excuses. Meanwhile, some of those who already had a scripture recognised the new one immediately and believed. The difference was not in the amount of evidence but in the honesty of the heart. The Quran is teaching that recognition of truth depends far more on sincerity than on proof. A humble, faithful soul sees the light at once, while a proud and self-interested one can be surrounded by signs and still miss them all.",
  "53": "“When it is recited to them, they declare, We believe in it. This is definitely the truth from our Lord. We had already submitted even before this.” These words give us the voice of those faithful believers themselves. When the Quran was recited to them, their response was immediate and wholehearted. There was no hesitation, no bargaining, no demand for further proof. They simply said, “We believe in it,” because they recognised the familiar voice of the same Lord who had spoken to them before.\n\n“This is definitely the truth from our Lord.” Notice the certainty. They do not say this might be true, or that it is probably true. They are sure. Their certainty came from preparation. Because they had lived honestly by the truth they already possessed, they had developed a kind of inner sight. When genuine revelation appeared, they could identify it the way a trained ear identifies a real voice among imitations. Faithfulness had sharpened their discernment until truth was unmistakable to them.\n\n“We had already submitted even before this.” This is the key to their story. They were not converting to something brand new. They were continuing a submission that had begun long before. The Arabic word “islam” means submission to God, and these believers are saying that their lives had already been shaped by surrender to the One God. Hearing the Quran was not a departure from their old faith; it was the completion of it. The same submission now found its final form.\n\nThere is a deep lesson here about the unity of true religion. From the first human being to the last prophet, the core of every authentic message has been the same: submit to the One God, live righteously, and prepare for the meeting with Him. The rituals and laws differed from age to age, but the heart of the matter never changed. Those who grasped that one truth in their own time had no difficulty recognising it when it returned. Their example is an encouragement to every reader: the more sincerely a person lives by whatever truth they have been given, the more ready they will be to receive whatever further light God sends, and the more surely they will recognise it when it comes.",
  "54": "“These believers will be given a double reward for their perseverance, responding to evil with good, and for donating from what We have provided for them.” This verse promises something striking to those faithful believers: a double reward. The idea of a doubled portion appears in several authentic reports and companion explanations. One common understanding is that they earned reward twice over, once for believing faithfully in the scripture they were first given, and again for recognising and following the Quran when it came. Their lifelong loyalty was rewarded, and so was their openness to new guidance.\n\nThe reward is first tied to “their perseverance.” Faithfulness of this kind does not come easily. It costs patience to hold on to truth when the surrounding society drifts, to keep believing when belief is mocked, and to wait faithfully for the final messenger. The Quran honours that hidden, stubborn endurance. Real faith is often a long act of patience long before it becomes a moment of joy.\n\nThen comes a beautiful phrase: “responding to evil with good.” These believers did not merely endure; they answered harm with kindness. When people were harsh or ignorant toward them, they repaid it with generosity and grace. This is one of the highest virtues the Quran praises, and it is far harder than simple retaliation. To meet injury with good requires a heart that is free of bitterness and rooted in God. It is a sign of genuine strength, not weakness.\n\nThe third quality is generosity: “donating from what We have provided for them.” Notice that even their wealth is described as something “We have provided.” They recognised that whatever they had was a trust from God, and so giving it away was not a loss but a returning. A believer who truly grasps that all provision comes from Allah gives freely, without fear of poverty, because they know the real Source can never run dry.\n\nTogether these three qualities, perseverance, answering evil with good, and giving from one’s provision, paint a portrait of mature faith. The double reward is not arbitrary favouritism; it is the natural fruit of a life lived with extraordinary sincerity and kindness. The verse invites every reader to measure themselves against these qualities and to ask which of them they are cultivating in their own heart.",
  "55": "“When they hear slanderous talk, they turn away from it.” This verse adds another layer to the portrait of those faithful believers. Their goodness showed itself not only in big virtues but in small, daily habits. One of those habits was the way they handled idle, foolish, or malicious speech. When such talk reached their ears, they did not stop to argue, they did not linger to enjoy it, and they did not pass it on. They simply turned away.\n\nThis is a quiet but powerful form of piety. It is easy to be drawn into gossip, scandal, and pointless debate. Such speech has a strange pull; it flatters the ego to hear others criticised and to feel superior. The believers described here had trained themselves to resist that pull. Their turning away was an act of self-respect and of respect for the dignity of speech itself. They understood that what a person listens to slowly shapes what they become, so they guarded their ears as carefully as others guard their wealth.\n\nThey explained themselves with remarkable grace: “We are accountable for our deeds and you for yours. Peace is our only response to you! We want nothing to do with those who act ignorantly.” There is no hostility in these words, only clarity. They take responsibility for their own actions and leave others to theirs. They offer peace rather than conflict. And they name what they are avoiding, not the people themselves, but ignorant behaviour.\n\nThe word “peace” here is significant. It echoes the greeting of Muslims and carries the sense of safety, wholeness, and freedom from conflict. Rather than answer foolishness with foolishness, they answer it with peace. This is the Quran teaching a high art of human relations: you do not have to participate in every argument you are invited to, you do not have to match every insult, and you can disagree completely while still wishing well. For any reader trying to navigate a world full of noise, outrage, and gossip, this verse offers a practical and dignified path: turn away from what corrupts, tend to your own account, and meet ignorance with a calm and sincere peace.",
  "56": "“You surely cannot guide whoever you like, O Prophet, but it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills.” This is one of the most beloved and sobering verses in the Quran. It speaks to the Prophet’s deep longing that those he loved should believe. He cared passionately about the people close to him and grieved when they refused the message. This verse gently corrects any idea that even a prophet can place faith in another person’s heart by sheer love or effort.\n\nAuthentic reports connect this verse to the Prophet’s grief over his beloved uncle Abu Talib, who had protected him for years but did not embrace Islam. The Prophet longed for his uncle’s guidance with all his heart, yet the man died holding to the old ways of his people. The verse was revealed to comfort and instruct: true guidance, the kind that transforms the soul, is in God’s hand alone. A messenger’s duty is to deliver the message clearly and to pray, but the final outcome belongs to Allah.\n\nThis teaches something essential about the nature of guidance. Guidance here is not merely information. Plenty of people hear the message and understand it intellectually. The guidance spoken of in this verse is the opening of the heart, the inward turning that makes a person actually embrace and live by the truth. That is a gift only God can give, and He grants it according to His perfect knowledge.\n\n“And He knows best who are fit to be guided.” The verse closes by placing the matter entirely in divine wisdom. God sees what no human can see: the secret intentions, the hidden readiness or unreadiness, the entire inner landscape of a soul. A person may seem close to faith yet lack the sincere turning of the heart; another may seem far away yet be ready to receive. Only God knows. This frees the believer from two equal errors: despairing when someone they love does not believe, and becoming arrogant when they themselves do. Both guidance and its absence are ultimately in wiser hands than ours, and the believer’s task is simply to share the message sincerely and trust the outcome to the One who knows best.",
  "57": "“They say to the Prophet, If we were to follow true guidance with you, we would certainly be snatched away from our land.” The leaders of Mecca offered a practical excuse for refusing faith. They argued that if they abandoned the religion of their forefathers and followed Muhammad, the surrounding tribes would turn on them and drive them from the sacred city. Fear of losing their home, their trade, and their security became a convenient shield behind which they hid their real reluctance.\n\nThe Quran answers this excuse by pointing to a fact they preferred to forget: “Have We not established for them a safe haven in Mecca to which fruits of all kinds are brought as a provision from Us?” Mecca was already a miracle of security. In a harsh land of raids and tribal warfare, its people lived in unusual peace, a peace that existed only because God had made the sanctuary sacred. Caravans travelled safely to and from it, and fruits from every direction were brought to a valley that grew none of its own. Their security was not their own achievement; it was a gift.\n\n“But most of them do not know this favour.” The tragic line is that they enjoyed the gift while forgetting the Giver. They took the safety of Mecca for granted, as if they had produced it themselves, and then used fear for that very safety as a reason to reject the One who had granted it. This is one of the most common spiritual failures: to live constantly inside God’s blessings and yet never connect them to God.\n\nThe argument cuts through their excuse completely. The same Lord who had already made their city a secure refuge, who fed them in a barren valley, was now calling them to guidance. Why would the One who sustained their bodies abandon them if they entrusted their souls to Him? Their fear was irrational, because it measured God’s protection as if it were small. The verse teaches a lasting lesson: genuine faith is never the cause of true loss, and the person who walks in obedience to God does not thereby lose His care. On the contrary, turning away from the Giver is the real danger, because every security we enjoy rests in hands we cannot see.",
  "58": "“Imagine how many societies We have destroyed that had been spoiled by their comfortable living!” This verse issues a sharp warning drawn from the ruins of history. It speaks of entire civilisations that once flourished, grew wealthy, and then vanished. Their downfall is linked directly to being “spoiled by their comfortable living.” Prosperity, when it breeds arrogance and heedlessness, becomes not a blessing but a trap. A society that grows soft and self-satisfied, that begins to believe its comfort is its own achievement and owes nothing to God, has already started down a dangerous road.\n\nThe Quran does not condemn wealth itself. Elsewhere it describes wealth as a provision and a trust. What it condemns is the spiritual corruption that comfort so often breeds: ingratitude, excess, cruelty toward the weak, and a stubborn forgetfulness of the One who gave it all. Many of the destroyed nations the Quran mentions were powerful and rich before their ruin. Their wealth did not save them; in a real sense it helped undo them, because it fooled them into thinking they were untouchable.\n\n“Those are their residences, never inhabited after them except passingly.” The verse points to the empty houses and fallen palaces left behind. A home that once echoed with laughter and planning stands silent. Travellers pass through but no one settles there again. There is something haunting in this image, the sheer fragility of human grandeur. The places that felt so permanent now stand as monuments to the truth that no civilisation, however mighty, is guaranteed tomorrow.\n\n“And We alone were the Successor.” The verse closes with a profound statement. After a people vanish, God remains, inheriting the earth and everything on it. Wealth, buildings, and empires all pass from hand to hand, but the true and lasting Owner never changes. This is both a warning and a comfort. It warns the powerful that their hold is temporary and their accounts are open. And it comforts the weak, who may feel that the wealthy and unjust will rule forever, by reminding them that every earthly possession ultimately returns to the One who lent it. The empty houses of the past speak a clear message: live with gratitude, use your ease to do good rather than to grow arrogant, and never mistake comfort for security.",
  "59": "“Your Lord would never destroy a society until He had sent to its capital a messenger, reciting Our revelations to them.” This verse states one of the great principles of divine justice. God does not wipe out a people without first sending them a clear warning. Destruction never falls on a nation that has had no chance to understand and turn back. A messenger is sent to the very heart of the society, its “capital,” the centre of its power and culture, so that the message reaches everyone from the leaders down.\n\nThis principle protects the fairness of God from any possible accusation. No one can say on the Day of Judgment, “We were destroyed without being warned.” The warning always comes first, delivered by a human being who lives among the people, who speaks their language, who shares their struggles, and who recites to them clear revelations. The choice is then placed fully in their hands.\n\n“Nor would We ever destroy a society unless its people persisted in wrongdoing.” Warning is followed by a period of patience. God gives time. He sends signs, He sends reminders, He withholds punishment year after year, giving every opportunity for repentance. Destruction arrives only when the wrongdoing has become persistent and entrenched, when the society as a whole has settled into corruption and refused to turn back despite every chance.\n\nThis verse directly answers a question that the people of Mecca were asking. They had been challenging the Prophet to bring down the punishment if he was truthful. The verse explains why the punishment had not fallen: the warning was still being delivered, and the door of repentance was still open. God’s patience with Mecca was not weakness; it was mercy in action, giving them time to believe.\n\nThe lesson reaches far beyond ancient nations. It shows that divine punishment is never impulsive or arbitrary. It follows a clear, just sequence: a messenger is sent, the truth is made plain, time is given, and only then, when wrongdoing has become a settled habit of the society, does the consequence arrive. This should make every person and every community thoughtful. The continued delay of consequences is not proof of safety; it is an opportunity to change. And the duty of those who carry the message is simply to deliver it clearly and patiently, leaving the outcome, and the timing, to the One whose justice is perfect.",
  "60": "“Whatever pleasure you have been given is no more than a fleeting enjoyment and adornment of this worldly life.” This verse turns the reader’s eyes toward the true value of worldly comforts. Every delight a person receives in this life, wealth, status, fine food, beautiful surroundings, is described with two telling words. It is a “fleeting enjoyment,” something that passes quickly, and it is an “adornment,” a decoration rather than the substance of life. The Quran does not deny that these things are real or that they give pleasure. It simply puts them in their proper place: temporary, decorative, and never to be mistaken for the goal of existence.\n\nThe phrase “of this worldly life” is important. The enjoyment is real, but it belongs entirely to the lower life, the life that ends. A person who builds their entire happiness on something that must disappear is building on sand. This is not a call to reject all comfort; it is a call to see comfort clearly, to enjoy it with gratitude without becoming enslaved to it.\n\n“But what is with Allah is far better and more lasting.” Here is the contrast that gives the verse its force. What God has prepared for those who turn to Him is described with two opposite qualities. It is “better,” meaning richer, purer, and more deeply satisfying than anything this world can offer, and it is “more lasting,” free from the decay and loss that touch everything here. The believer is being invited to make a sensible exchange: to give up an anxious clinging to what is temporary in order to gain what is permanent and superior.\n\n“Will you not then understand?” The verse ends as a gentle challenge. This is not deep philosophy that only scholars can grasp. It is plain arithmetic. A fleeting thing weighed against a lasting one, an adornment weighed against a true reward. Anyone who reflects honestly can see which is the wiser investment. The problem is never really a lack of understanding; it is the heart’s reluctance to let go of what it can see for what it cannot yet see. The verse appeals to that very faculty of understanding, asking the reader to look past the glitter of the moment and to judge with a clear head what truly deserves their effort and their love.",
  "61": "“Can those to whom We have made a fine promise—which they will see fulfilled—be like those who We have allowed to enjoy the pleasures of this worldly life, but on the Day of Judgment will be brought for punishment?” This verse sets two kinds of people side by side and asks whether they can possibly be equal. The question expects a clear no. On one side are those who have received from God a “fine promise,” a beautiful assurance of reward, and that promise is guaranteed, “which they will see fulfilled.” On the other side are those who were allowed to enjoy the pleasures of this world for a time, only to be dragged forward on the Day of Judgment for punishment.\n\nThe structure of the verse teaches a profound lesson about appearances. In this life, the second group may look enviable. They have wealth, comfort, and ease. The first group may look ordinary or even struggling. But the verse strips away the surface and shows the two outcomes that are coming. The one with the fine promise is heading toward lasting joy; the one with worldly pleasure is heading toward accountability. To judge them by their present circumstances alone is to misread everything.\n\nNotice the careful wording about the promise: it “will be seen fulfilled.” God’s promises are not hopes or maybes. They are certainties. The reward promised to the faithful is as sure as anything can be. This is meant to steady the heart of every believer who feels that doing the right thing costs them in this life. The verse assures them that no good deed, no act of restraint, no sacrifice made for God’s sake is forgotten. The account is kept perfectly, and the payment is certain.\n\nThe verse belongs to the flow of this passage, which keeps contrasting the temporary with the eternal. The previous verse called worldly pleasure a fleeting adornment. This one deepens the contrast by placing two whole lives and two whole destinies next to each other. It is a call to live with the end in view. The person who can see the Day of Judgment clearly will not trade a guaranteed eternal reward for a few years of comfort that end in loss. Such a person understands that true success is not measured by what one accumulates here, but by where one stands when this life is over and the truth of every promise is finally unveiled.",
  "62": "“Watch for the Day He will call to them, Where are those you claimed were My associate-gods?” This verse paints one of the most striking scenes of the Day of Judgment. God will address those who associated partners with Him, and His question will be simple and devastating: where are the gods you claimed beside Me? The question is not asked because God lacks knowledge. It is asked to expose, before all of creation, the emptiness of the idols these people had invented.\n\nThe phrase “you claimed” carries a quiet weight. It does not say “My associate-gods,” as if such partners truly existed. It says “those you claimed were My associate-gods.” The claiming was entirely theirs. God had no partner; they merely alleged one. On that Day the allegation collapses. The idols, whether statues, saints, worldly powers, or desires that had been raised to the level of worship, will be nowhere to be found. The question itself becomes their humiliation, because the silence that follows is the answer.\n\nThere is a progression worth noticing across these verses. The previous verses contrasted the destinies of the faithful and those who cling to worldly pleasure. Now the camera moves to the moment of judgment itself, and the first thing shown is this interrogation of the idolaters. The Quran returns to this same scene several times in this passage, each time drawing out a different detail. The repetition is deliberate, because the shock of that Day, when every false support vanishes, is something the human heart needs to feel in advance in order to wake up.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a searching question posed now, while there is still time. Where are the things I am tempted to worship besides God? Can they help me? Can they hear me? Will they stand by me on the Day when nothing remains but the truth? The idols of every age, whether made of stone or of money, status, or ambition, share the same fatal flaw: they cannot answer when they are truly needed. The verse invites each person to examine their own loyalties and to place their trust only in the One who will still be present, and still in command, on that awesome Day.",
  "63": "“Those misleaders against whom the decree of torment is justified will cry, Our Lord! These followers are the ones we caused to deviate.” This verse reveals a bitter moment on the Day of Judgment. The leaders who led others astray, the figures who in this life commanded loyalty and admiration, will turn on their own followers. Instead of defending the people who trusted them, they will hand them over. “These are the ones we caused to deviate,” they will confess, desperate to shift the blame away from themselves.\n\nThis is one of the great warnings of the Quran about whom a person chooses to follow. In this life, following a popular leader, a fashionable ideology, or a charismatic figure can feel safe simply because the crowd is doing it. But the verse exposes the reality behind such loyalty. The leaders never truly loved their followers; they used them. And when the danger becomes real, on the Day when excuses dissolve, those leaders will be the first to disown the very people they misled. “We led them into deviance, for we ourselves were deviant,” they admit, and then, in the same breath, “We disassociate ourselves from them before You.”\n\n“It was not us that they used to worship.” This final claim is staggering in its dishonesty. The leaders try to argue that since the followers were really worshipping their own desires rather than the leaders themselves, the leaders should not be held responsible. It is a last, frantic attempt to escape. The verse shows how thoroughly selfishness unravels every bond of false allegiance. The loyalty that was demanded in this life is revealed as hollow; the bond breaks the instant it costs something.\n\nRead against the previous verse, the contrast is sharp. A few verses earlier, the Quran spoke of those to whom God made “a fine promise.” Here we see the opposite: bonds built on misguidance collapsing into mutual betrayal. The lesson reaches every reader. Be careful whom you take as a guide, and be careful whom you lead. The follower who chooses a guide for any reason other than genuine truth is walking toward a day of abandonment, and the leader who misuses trust is storing up a day of disgrace. True loyalty, the kind that endures, is built only on shared devotion to the truth and to the One who will never disown those who turn to Him sincerely.",
  "64": "“It will be said to the disbelievers, Call upon your associate-gods for help.” This verse continues the scene of that Day with a moment of painful irony. The very idols and partners that the disbelievers had relied upon in life will be put to the test. They will be told, in effect: go ahead, call upon those you used to worship besides God. Let us see what they can do for you now, when the punishment has arrived.\n\n“So they will call them, but will receive no response.” The call will go up, and the answer will be silence. Every statue, every saint, every power besides God that was supposed to protect and provide will prove utterly helpless. This is the moment of truth that exposes all idolatry. In this life, the delay of consequences can make a false hope look reasonable. A person may pray to something other than God and, because nothing bad happens immediately, conclude that their hope was well-placed. On that Day the illusion ends. The false gods cannot hear, cannot speak, and cannot save.\n\n“And they will face the punishment, wishing they had been rightly guided!” The verse captures the depth of their regret. Faced with the reality they denied, their one wish will be to have been guided. Notice the word “wishing.” Guidance was offered to them in this life, freely and clearly, and they refused it. Now, when the consequences are visible, they ache for it, but the time for choosing has passed. This is one of the saddest refrains in the Quran: the wish for guidance that comes too late.\n\nThe verse follows naturally from the one before it. The previous verse showed the false leaders disowning their followers. This verse shows the false gods failing to answer. Every false support, human or divine, collapses in the same way. The lesson for the living is unmistakable. The time to test your hopes is now, not later. Whatever a person trusts besides God should be honestly examined today: can it hear me? Can it help me in my darkest moment? Will it stand by me when I truly need it? The Quran’s answer is consistent and clear: only the One God can. To place ultimate trust in anything else is to walk toward a day of silence, regret, and a wish for guidance that can no longer help.",
  "65": "“And watch for the Day He will call to them, asking, What response did you give to the messengers?” This is the second great question of that Day. The first, in an earlier verse, asked about the false gods they had invented: where are they? This one asks about the true messengers they had been sent: how did you answer them? The shift is significant. Idolatry is exposed by the absence of the idols, but unbelief is exposed by the record of how a person treated the guidance that actually reached them.\n\nThe question “What response did you give to the messengers?” is searching because it concerns the heart of human responsibility. Every people received a warner. The messengers came with clear signs and a plain call. The only thing left to the listeners was their response. Some believed and followed; others mocked, ignored, or fought against the message. On that Day, that response will be laid open. There will be no hiding behind ignorance, because the messengers had delivered the truth clearly.\n\nNotice that the question is asked of everyone. It is not only the rejecters who are questioned; the believers too will give their answer, and theirs will be a response of faith and gratitude. The verse thus holds up a mirror to every soul. How have I responded to the truth I have been given? Have I listened, believed, and acted? Or have I turned away, made excuses, and delayed? The answer a person is forming right now, in this life, is the answer they will carry into that Day.\n\nThis verse deepens the theme running through the whole passage. Again and again the Quran has spoken of messengers being sent, of warnings being delivered, of the justice of holding people accountable only after the truth has reached them. Here the logic reaches its climax: because the messengers came, no one can plead ignorance; and because the response was each person’s own choice, each person bears their own outcome. For the believer, this verse is both a sober reminder and a quiet encouragement. Sober, because every response to truth is recorded. Encouraging, because the One asking the question is the same Lord who, in His mercy, sent the messengers in the first place, giving every soul the chance to answer rightly and to enter His forgiveness and pleasure.",
  "66": "“They will be too dumbstruck on that Day to ask one another for answers.” This verse captures the sheer shock of the Day of Judgment. People who in this life never stopped talking, arguing, justifying, and explaining will be struck silent. The word “dumbstruck” paints a picture of a person so overwhelmed that speech fails them entirely. Every clever argument they once used to deny the truth, every excuse they invented, every smooth reply they gave to the messengers, all of it will evaporate. There will be nothing left to say.\n\n“They will be too dumbstruck... to ask one another for answers.” In this life, people constantly turn to one another for support in their denial. They reassure each other, reinforce each other’s doubts, and congratulate each other on being clever. On that Day that whole network of mutual encouragement will collapse. No one will be able to help anyone else think of an answer, because no one will have one. The solidarity of unbelief, which looked so strong in the world, will be revealed as empty.\n\nThe timing of this silence is important. The previous verse described the great question: “What response did you give to the messengers?” This verse shows the reaction to that question. Confronted with the truth they spent a lifetime avoiding, the deniers will have no reply. Their silence itself becomes the verdict. In this life they had plenty to say against the truth; in the presence of the truth made fully visible, they are left speechless.\n\nThis scene is a powerful commentary on the nature of worldly argument. Much of what passes for confidence in unbelief is really noise, the constant chatter of people reassuring one another that they are right. Strip away the social support and the bravado, and there is nothing beneath it. The Quran invites the reader to imagine standing in that silence, with nothing to lean on and nothing to say, and to let that imagination do its work now. The person who grasps the reality of that Day while there is still time will not wait to be silenced by it. They will speak the truth now, believe now, and act now, so that on the Day when speech fails the deniers, they will instead be among those whose record speaks for them in the best possible way.",
  "67": "“As for those who repent, believe, and do good in this world, it is right to hope that they will be among the successful.” After the terrifying scenes of the previous verses, the Quran opens a door of mercy. Not everyone is destined for the silence and regret just described. There is a way out, and it is described in three simple steps: repent, believe, and do good. These three actions together form the complete path of return to God.\n\nThe first step is repentance. The Arabic word “tawbah” means to turn back, to return. It is not merely feeling guilty; it is an active turning of the heart away from sin and toward God, accompanied by regret and a resolve to change. Repentance is the door through which a person re-enters the path after having strayed. No matter how far someone has wandered, this door is always open in this life. The verse places it first because without turning back, the other steps cannot truly begin.\n\nThe second step is belief. Repentance must be joined to genuine faith, a settled trust in God, His messengers, and the truth He has revealed. Faith is the foundation. Without it, good deeds are like a beautiful building with no base. And the third step is doing good. Faith is not meant to stay hidden in the heart; it is meant to flow out into action. A person who truly believes will naturally begin to act differently, treating people well, giving, serving, and standing for what is right.\n\n“It is right to hope that they will be among the successful.” Notice the gentle, hopeful tone. The verse does not say they will definitely succeed as a cold guarantee; it says it is “right to hope.” This phrasing balances two truths. On one hand, the person who repents, believes, and does good has every reason to be hopeful, because God’s mercy is vast and His promise is true. On the other hand, no believer should become complacent or arrogant about their salvation. The believer lives between hope and humility, trusting God’s mercy while never taking it for granted.\n\nThis verse is one of the great verses of hope in the Quran. Right after describing the doom of the deniers, it extends mercy to anyone willing to turn. The contrast is itself a message: the path to destruction is chosen, but the path to success is open to all who will take these three steps. For any reader who feels weighed down by past mistakes, this verse is an invitation and a reassurance. The door is not closed. Return is possible. Success belongs, by God’s grace, to those who turn back to Him with a sincere heart.",
  "68": "“Your Lord creates and chooses whatever He wills—the choice is not theirs.” This verse establishes one of the foundations of how a believer understands the world. The right to choose, in the deepest sense, belongs to God alone. He creates what He wills, and He selects what He wills, whether it concerns prophets, nations, circumstances, or the unfolding of events. Human beings do not get to dictate how reality should be arranged, nor do they have the authority to tell God whom He should have chosen as a messenger or how He should guide His creation.\n\nThe verse was particularly relevant to the situation in Mecca. The powerful leaders of the Quraysh felt that prophethood, if it was to come at all, should have come to one of their great chiefs, someone wealthy and influential, rather than to Muhammad, who was, in their eyes, an ordinary man without a large following. Their objection was really a complaint against God’s choice. This verse answers them directly: the choice is not yours to make. God selects whom He wills, and His wisdom is beyond the petty standards of wealth and status that humans use to judge one another.\n\n“Glorified and Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!” The verse closes with a declaration of God’s perfection. He is far above the partners and rivals that people invent. To suggest that God needs human approval for His choices, or that He should consult the powerful of the earth before appointing a prophet, is to lower Him to the level of His creatures. The verse lifts the heart back to a true vision of God: the One who creates and chooses in perfect freedom, answerable to no one, partner to none.\n\nThere is a deep comfort in this teaching for every believer. So much of human frustration comes from wanting to control outcomes that are not ours to control, who receives guidance, how events turn out, what others think of us. This verse releases the believer from that burden. The choices that truly matter belong to the All-Wise, and He exercises them with perfect knowledge and mercy. The believer’s role is not to manage the universe but to trust the One who does, to submit to His decisions, and to find peace in the knowledge that the Lord who creates and chooses all things does so with a wisdom far greater than any human mind could devise.",
  "69": "“And your Lord knows what their hearts conceal and what they reveal.” This verse states a truth that is both sobering and comforting: nothing about any human being is hidden from God. He knows the inner life, the secrets buried in the heart, the motives no one else can see, as completely as He knows the words and deeds that are out in the open. The hidden and the visible are equally plain to Him.\n\nThis knowledge is mentioned immediately after the previous verse, which declared that God creates and chooses whatever He wills. The connection is significant. God’s choices are not arbitrary; they are made with perfect knowledge. When He selects a prophet, guides a heart, or determines an outcome, He does so knowing the full reality of every person involved. Unlike human judges, who can be fooled by appearances and misled by smooth words, God sees to the very core. A person may fool everyone else with a pious outward show, but the heart behind it is fully exposed to Him. Likewise, a person may be misunderstood or maligned by people, but the sincere truth within is known and valued by their Lord.\n\nFor the believer, this is a source of both caution and comfort. The caution is clear: since God sees the secrets, hypocrisy is futile. A faith that exists only on the surface, with no reality in the heart, is seen for what it is. Hidden sins, private resentments, and concealed intentions are all recorded. This should keep the believer honest, not just in public but in the privacy of their own soul. The comfort is equally real: every sincere intention, every quiet act of goodness done with no witness but God, every pain endured privately for His sake, is known and will be rewarded. Nothing good is ever lost, because the One who matters most has seen it all.\n\nThis verse also exposes the futility of the deniers’ strategies. The people of Mecca plotted in secret against the Prophet and the believers, then presented a different face in public. They might deceive one another, but they could never deceive God. Their concealed hatred and their open mockery were both laid bare before Him. The verse stands as a quiet reassurance to every oppressed believer that no injustice goes unseen, and as a gentle warning to every soul that the true record is the one kept by the One who knows both the hidden and the revealed.",
  "70": "“He is Allah. There is no god worthy of worship except Him.” This verse gathers the central creed of Islam into a single, powerful declaration. After the long stories of Moses and Pharaoh, Korah and the doubters of Mecca, the chapter turns to state plainly who God is. He is Allah, the One true God, and there is no deity deserving of worship besides Him. Every other claim to divinity, whether Pharaoh’s self-deification or the idols of the Arabs, is swept away by this single sentence.\n\n“All praise belongs to Him in this life and the next.” The verse expands the scope of praise to cover all of existence. In this life, every good thing a person experiences, every breath, every provision, every moment of peace, ultimately calls for gratitude to the One who gave it. And in the next life, the believers will praise Him for His justice and His mercy, while even those who denied Him will, in the end, acknowledge that all praise belonged to Him alone. Praise is not confined to a prayer or a moment; it is the rightful posture of the entire creation toward its Creator, now and forever.\n\n“All authority is His.” This phrase cuts through every illusion of human power. In this chapter we have seen Pharaoh claim authority, Haman build his tower, Korah flaunt his wealth, and the leaders of Mecca strut with pride. This verse reminds the reader that all such authority was borrowed and temporary. Real authority, the kind that decides the ultimate fate of every soul, belongs to God alone. Every tyrant who ever lived has already discovered, or will discover, how thin their power really was.\n\n“And to Him you will all be returned.” The verse closes with the great fact that gives all the others their weight: every human being is going back to God. This return is certain, and it is the moment when the truth of the creed, the praise, and the authority will become fully visible. The whole chapter has been building toward this point. The stories of destruction and deliverance, of arrogance and humility, of warning and promise, all converge here on the One to whom everyone returns. For the believer, this verse is a summary of everything worth holding onto: worship only Him, praise Him always, recognise His authority, and prepare for the meeting with Him, because that meeting is the one appointment no one will miss.",
  "71": "“Ask them, O Prophet, Imagine if Allah were to make the night perpetual for you until the Day of Judgment, which god other than Allah could bring you sunlight?” This verse invites the listener to perform a simple but powerful act of imagination. Suppose the night were made to last forever, never giving way to dawn. Who, besides Allah, could possibly bring back the light of day? The question is rhetorical because the answer is obvious: no one. The alternation of night and day, which people take for granted as simply the way things are, is revealed as a continuous gift that depends entirely on God.\n\nThe genius of this argument is that it uses something so familiar that people have stopped seeing it. Every single day, the night ends and the sun rises. This happens so reliably that it feels automatic, like a law of nature that runs on its own. But the verse asks the reader to imagine that gift withdrawn. If the night did not end, who could fix it? Could the idols answer? Could the powerful leaders of Mecca command the sun? The silence that meets these questions exposes the truth: the One who gives the sunrise is the only One worthy of worship.\n\n“Will you not then listen?” The verse ends with a call to pay attention. The evidence is not obscure. It is written across the sky every single morning. The problem is not that God has hidden Himself; it is that human beings have grown deaf to the message constantly spoken by creation. To “listen” here means more than hearing sounds. It means opening the heart to the truth that is being declared by the very rhythm of the world, and responding with gratitude and worship toward the One who sustains it.\n\nThis verse belongs to a passage that keeps pointing to the signs of God in the natural world. After establishing the creed in the previous verse, the Quran immediately turns to creation as proof. It is a reminder that faith is not a leap in the dark. It is a response to overwhelming evidence that surrounds us at every moment. The person who reflects on the ordinary miracles of day and night, sun and rain, life and growth, finds in them a continuous invitation to know the One who made them, to trust Him, and to worship Him alone, before the day comes when the chance to listen has passed.",
  "72": "“Ask them also, Imagine if Allah were to make the day perpetual for you until the Day of Judgment, which god other than Allah could bring you night to rest in?” This verse mirrors the one before it, but from the opposite direction. The previous verse asked about an endless night; this one asks about an endless day. Suppose the blazing sun never set, and there was no night in which to find rest and coolness. Who, besides Allah, could bring the covering of night? Again the answer is clear: no one but God.\n\nThe pairing of these two verses teaches an important balance. Human life depends on both day and night. The day brings light for work and activity, the night brings rest and refreshment. Take away either one permanently, and life would become unbearable. The Quran invites the reader to notice that this delicate, life-sustaining rhythm is not a happy accident. It is the continuous provision of a wise and merciful Creator, who tailored the world precisely for the creatures He placed within it.\n\nNotice the gentle word “rest.” The night is described as a mercy, a time when the exhausted body and mind can recover. This shows the tender care built into the design of creation. God did not merely make a world that functions; He made one that is kind to its inhabitants. The cycle of day and night is a daily pattern of grace, repeating over and over so that no one should ever be far from a reminder of their Lord’s goodness.\n\n“Will you not then see?” The closing question calls for a different faculty than the previous verse. There it was “listen”; here it is “see.” The Quran uses both, because the signs of God address every sense and every kind of heart. Some people are moved by what they hear, others by what they observe. The verses between them cover both paths. For the reader, the message is an invitation to wake up the senses that have gone dull through routine, to look at the ordinary world with fresh eyes, and to recognise in the turning of day into night and night into day the steady handwriting of the One who alone can give light and rest, and who alone is worthy of all worship and gratitude.",
  "73": "“It is out of His mercy that He has made for you the day and night so that you may rest in the latter and seek His bounty in the former, and perhaps you will be grateful.” This verse explains the purpose behind the cycle of day and night that the previous two verses described. The alternation is not random; it is a gift of mercy, carefully designed to serve human life. The night is given for rest, a time when the body recovers and the mind finds peace. The day is given for seeking “His bounty,” for work, provision, and the pursuit of all that sustains life.\n\nThe phrase “out of His mercy” is the heart of the verse. Everything in creation, from the grand motion of the heavens to the small comfort of sleep, flows from the mercy of God. He did not owe human beings a world tailored to their needs. He provided it freely, as an act of kindness. Recognising this transforms the way a person experiences ordinary life. Sleep becomes a mercy rather than a routine. Work becomes a seeking of God’s bounty rather than mere survival. The whole rhythm of existence becomes a continuous conversation between the Creator’s generosity and the creature’s gratitude.\n\nThe verse names the response God is looking for: “perhaps you will be grateful.” Gratitude is the natural answer to mercy. When a person truly sees that every good thing comes from God, the heart fills with thankfulness, and that thankfulness shows itself in how they live. A grateful person uses God’s gifts well, shares them with others, and never forgets the Giver. The word “perhaps” keeps the door of choice open, just as we have seen elsewhere in this chapter. The mercy is given; gratitude is the response God hopes for, but it cannot be forced. It must rise freely from a heart that has learned to see.\n\nThis verse beautifully softens the tone after the searching questions of the previous two. Those verses challenged the listener to think about who controls day and night. This verse reveals the loving purpose behind that control. The same God who could make the night or day perpetual instead chose a gentle rhythm of rest and work, all for the sake of His creatures. It is an invitation to trust such a Lord, to receive each day and each night as a fresh gift, and to let a life of steady gratitude be the answer to a mercy that never stops.",
  "74": "“And watch for the Day He will call to them, Where are those you claimed were My associate-gods?” This verse repeats, almost word for word, a question that appeared earlier in this same passage. That earlier verse asked the identical question of the idolaters on the Day of Judgment. The repetition is not accidental. The Quran returns to this scene deliberately, because the shock of that Day, when every false god vanishes, is a truth the human heart needs to confront more than once in order to truly absorb it.\n\nThe fact that the same question comes twice, framing a whole section in between, gives the passage a powerful structure. The first time the question was asked, the verses that followed showed the false leaders disowning their followers and the false gods failing to answer. Now the question returns, and the verses that follow will show the witnesses being brought and the proof demanded. Each cycle adds a new layer to the same overwhelming reality: on that Day, nothing a person trusted besides God will be of any use.\n\nRepetition in the Quran always serves a purpose. It is not a sign of forgetfulness but a method of teaching. A truth stated once may be noted and then filed away. A truth that returns, in slightly different surroundings, forces the listener to stop and consider it again, this time more deeply. The Quran is not in a hurry. It is willing to circle back to the most important realities, because those realities are the difference between salvation and ruin, and they deserve to be pressed upon the heart again and again.\n\nFor the reader, the lesson of the repetition is personal. The question “Where are those you claimed were My associate-gods?” is being asked now, in advance, while there is still time to answer it honestly. A person can ask themselves today: what am I relying on besides God? Is it money, status, other people, my own cleverness? Can any of these stand by me on the Day when only the truth remains? The verse, by coming back a second time, gives the reader a second chance to take it seriously, to loosen every false loyalty now, and to place their trust wholly in the One God before that Day arrives and the question is no longer hypothetical but terrifyingly real.",
  "75": "“And We will bring forth a witness from every faith-community and ask the polytheists, Show Us your proof.” This verse describes a moment of perfect justice on the Day of Judgment. From every community that ever existed, a witness will be brought forward. These witnesses are generally understood to be the prophets and messengers who were sent to each people, or the record of truth established among them. Their testimony will confirm what was taught and how it was received. Then, before this complete and fair record, the polytheists will be challenged: show your proof. Produce the evidence that justified your worship of others besides God.\n\nThe demand for proof is devastating because there is no proof to give. The polytheists had no revelation from God commanding them to worship idols. They had no sound argument, no genuine sign, no truthful basis for the partners they invented. Their entire religion rested on custom, desire, and inherited habit. Faced with the demand for proof, they will fall silent, because the truth is that they never had any.\n\n“Then they will come to know that the truth is with Allah alone.” The verse describes a moment of reluctant, total recognition. Whatever doubts or denials they clung to in this life will dissolve. The truth, which they spent their lives resisting, will stand before them undeniable. This knowledge will bring them no joy, because it will have come too late to help them. It is the bitter knowledge of a person who finally sees clearly only when seeing can no longer save them.\n\n“And whatever gods they fabricated will fail them.” The verse closes by naming the final collapse. The word “fabricated” is key. These gods were not discovered; they were made up, fashioned by human imagination and hands. And now, exactly as one would expect of inventions, they fail. They cannot help, cannot intercede, cannot even acknowledge the people who served them. The whole apparatus of false religion, the temples, the rituals, the devotion, is exposed as a structure built on nothing.\n\nThis verse completes the movement of the whole passage. The false gods were summoned and gave no answer. The false leaders disowned their followers. Now the witnesses confirm the truth, the demand for proof exposes the emptiness, and the fabricated gods fail. Every false support has been tested and found worthless. The lesson, repeated in every possible form, is that only Allah is real, only His truth endures, and only turning to Him sincerely, before that Day, can save a person from the silence and failure that await those who trusted in anything else.",
  "76": "“Indeed, Korah was from the people of Moses, but he behaved arrogantly towards them. We had granted him such treasures that even their keys would burden a group of strong men. ˹Some of˺ his people advised him, “Do not be prideful! Surely Allah does not like the prideful.”” records Korah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Korah: or \"inna Qaroona kana min qawmi Moosa fa-bagha 'alayhim wa ataynahu mina al-kunoozi ma inna mafatihahu la-tanoo'u bil-'usbati oolee al-quwwati ith qala lahu qawmuhu la tafrah inna Allaha la yuhibbu al-fariheen,\" and the commentators note Korah, for Korah behaved arrogantly, and the verse is the record of Korah, the arrogance that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the treasures are the test, and the verse is the record of Korah, the arrogance of the rich. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the arrogance and the treasures are the test, and the verse is the record of Korah, the arrogance that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the wealth is the test, and the one who is arrogant is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the record of Korah is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the wealth: Korah was arrogant and swallowed, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the record is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of Korah, and the teaching of the humility that the wealth requires.",
  "77": "“Rather, seek the ˹reward˺ of the Hereafter by means of what Allah has granted you, without forgetting your share of this world. And be good ˹to others˺ as Allah has been good to you. Do not seek to spread corruption in the land, for Allah certainly does not like the corruptors.”” records the advice, and the commentators note the two parts of the advice. The verse records the advice: or \"wabtaghi feema ataka Allahu ad-dara al-akhirata wa la tansa naseebaka mina ad-dunya wa ahsin kama ahsana Allahu ilayka wa la tabghi al-fasada fi al-ardi inna Allaha la yuhibbu al-mufsideen,\" and the commentators note the advice, for the people advised Korah, and the advice is the teaching, the balance of the two worlds, and the verse is the record of the advice, the teaching that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the balance is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the advice, the teaching of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the advice is the teaching and the balance is the two worlds, and the verse is the record of the advice, the teaching that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the advice, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own balance. The believer is taught that the balance of the two worlds is the teaching, and the one who seeks the Hereafter is the one whose balance the Lord values, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose balance the Lord accepts, and the advice of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the balance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the advice is the believer whose balance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the two worlds: the people advised Korah, and the believer who seeks the Hereafter is the believer whose balance the Lord values, and the advice is the teaching that the balance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the advice, and the teaching of the balance that the wealth requires.",
  "78": "“He replied, “I have been granted all this because of some knowledge I have.” Did he not know that Allah had already destroyed some from the generations before him who were far superior to him in power and greater in accumulating ˹wealth˺? There will be no need for the wicked to be asked about their sins.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala innama ooteetuhu 'ala 'ilmin 'indee a-wa-lam ya'lam anna Allaha qad ahlaka min qablihi mina al-qurooni man huwa ashaddu minhu quwwatan wa aktharu jam'an wa la yus'alu 'an thunoobihimu al-mujrimoon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Korah claimed the knowledge, and the reply is the arrogance, the claim of the merit, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the claim is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance of Korah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the arrogance and the claim is the merit, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the claim of the merit is the arrogance, and the one who claims is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reply of Korah is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the claim: Korah claimed and was swallowed, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the humility that the claim requires.",
  "79": "“Then he came out before his people in all his glamour. Those who desired the life of this world wished, “If only we could have something like what Korah has been given. He is truly a man of great fortune!”” records the display, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the display: or \"fa-kharaja 'ala qawmihi fee zeenatihi qala allatheena yureedoona al-hayata ad-dunya ya layta lana mithla ma ootiya Qaroonu innahu la-thoo hazzin 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the display, for Korah displayed the glamour, and the display is the arrogance, the show of the wealth, and the verse is the record of the display, the arrogance that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the display is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the display, the arrogance of Korah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the display is the arrogance and the glamour is the show, and the verse is the record of the display, the arrogance that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the display, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the display of the wealth is the arrogance, and the one who displays is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the display of Korah is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the display is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the display: Korah displayed and was swallowed, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the display is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the display, and the teaching of the humility that the show requires.",
  "80": "“But those gifted with knowledge said, “Shame on you! Allah’s reward is far better for those who believe and do good. But none will attain this except the steadfast.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"wa qala allatheena ootoo al-'ilma waylakum thawabu Allahi khayrun liman amana wa 'amila salihan wa la yulaqqaha illa as-sabiroon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the knowledgeable rebuked the desire, and the reply is the correction, the declaration of the better, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the correction is the truth, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction of the knowledgeable. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the correction and the better is the reward, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the reward of the Lord is the better, and the one who knows is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of the knowledgeable is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the better of the reward: the knowledgeable corrected the desire, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the certainty that the correction produces.",
  "81": "“Then We caused the earth to swallow him up, along with his home. There was no one to help him against Allah, nor could he even help himself.”” records the swallowing, and the commentators note the two parts of the swallowing. The verse records the swallowing: or \"fa-khasafna bihi wa bidarihi al-arda fa-ma kana lahu min fi'atin yansuroonahu min dooni Allahi wa ma kana mina al-muntasireen,\" and the commentators note the swallowing, for the Lord caused the earth to swallow Korah, and the swallowing is the judgment, the end of the arrogant, and the verse is the record of the swallowing, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the swallowing is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the swallowing, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the swallowing is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the swallowing, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the swallowing, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who is arrogant is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the swallowing of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the swallowing is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: Korah was swallowed and had no helper, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the swallowing is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the swallowing, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "82": "“And those who had craved his position the previous day began to say, “Ah! It is certainly Allah Who gives abundant or limited provisions to whoever He wills of His servants. Had it not been for the grace of Allah, He could have surely caused the earth to swallow us up! Oh, indeed! The disbelievers will never succeed.”” records the reflection, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reflection: or \"wa asbaha allatheena tamannaw makanahu bil-amsi yaqooloona wayka-anna Allaha yabsutu ar-rizqa liman yasha'u min 'ibadihi wa yaqdiru law la an manna Allahu 'alayna la-khasafa bina wayka-annahu la yuflihu al-kafiroon,\" and the commentators note the reflection, for the people reflected and confessed, and the reflection is the lesson, the acknowledgment of the truth, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the lesson that the account closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the reflection is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the lesson of Korah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reflection is the lesson and the confession is the truth, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the lesson that the account closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reflection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the reflection upon the judgment is the lesson, and the one who reflects is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who learns from the history is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reflection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the reflection is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the lesson of Korah: the people reflected and learned, and the believer who learns from the history is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reflection is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the reflection, and the teaching of the certainty that the lesson produces.",
  "83": "“That eternal Home in the Hereafter We reserve only for those who seek neither tyranny nor corruption on the earth.” After the dramatic fall of Korah, this verse draws out the lasting principle behind his ruin. The “eternal Home,” Paradise, is reserved for a specific kind of person: one whose heart is free from the two diseases that destroyed Korah. Such a person does not seek “tyranny,” the arrogant desire to rise above others and dominate them, nor “corruption,” the spreading of harm and disorder in the land. The wealth and power that Korah pursued as goals are, for the believer, things to be handled with care and used for good, never craved as ends in themselves.\n\nThe word “eternal” is set against everything Korah trusted. His home, so solid and full of treasure, lasted only a day before the earth swallowed it. The Home God promises lasts forever. The contrast is the whole point. The person who lives for what is temporary, building their life on wealth and status, is investing in something that will be taken away. The person who lives for the eternal Home, keeping their heart humble and their hands clean of tyranny and corruption, is investing in something that will never be lost.\n\n“The ultimate outcome belongs only to the righteous.” This closing phrase seals the lesson. The “ultimate outcome,” the final result that truly matters, is not the one seen in the middle of the story. In the middle, Korah looked like the winner and the humble believers looked like losers. But the ultimate outcome belonged to the righteous, not to the rich. Righteousness, not wealth, is what wins in the end. This is the measure the Quran uses again and again, and the Korah story is its most vivid illustration.\n\nThis verse gathers up the entire chapter. From Pharaoh’s arrogance to Korah’s pride, from the doubters of Mecca to the humble believers, the same principle runs through every story: those who seek tyranny and corruption lose, even when they seem to win, and those who hold to righteousness gain the eternal Home, even when they seem to have nothing. For the reader, the verse is both a warning and a promise. It warns that the craving for dominance and the spreading of harm shut a person out of the eternal Home. And it promises that a humble, sincere, righteous life, however ordinary it looks, is the true path to the only success that will last forever.",
  "84": "“Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded with what is better.” This verse states the justice of the Hereafter in its purest form. On the Day of Judgment, every person will come with the record of what they have done. The one who brings a good deed will receive a reward that is even better than the deed itself. God does not merely pay back exactly what is owed; He, in His generosity, multiplies and exceeds it. A single sincere act of kindness, one honest prayer, one moment of patience, may return as a vast and lasting reward. This is the grace that makes the believer hopeful, the knowledge that even small goodness, offered sincerely, is met with abundant generosity.\n\n“And whoever comes with an evil deed, then the evildoers will only be rewarded for what they used to do.” Here is the perfect balance of divine justice. A person who comes with an evil deed will be repaid only for what they actually did, no more. God does not pile on extra punishment beyond what is deserved. The wicked receive exactly the wage of their wrongdoing, fair and measured. This dual principle, generosity heaped upon goodness and strict fairness applied to evil, is one of the most beautiful descriptions of God’s justice in the Quran. Mercy and justice meet without contradiction.\n\nThe contrast between the two halves of the verse is striking and intentional. Good is rewarded with “what is better,” more than its worth. Evil is rewarded only with “what they used to do,” exactly its worth and not a fraction more. The implication is profound: God’s dealings lean toward mercy. He finds ways to increase the good and limit the harm. A believer who grasps this is filled with hope, because they know their small efforts are met with large rewards. And a wrongdoer is left without excuse, because even their punishment is scrupulously fair.\n\nThis verse comes right after the declaration that the eternal Home belongs to the righteous. It explains why: because on the Day when deeds are weighed, righteousness is generously rewarded and wrongdoing is justly repaid. There is no arbitrariness, no favoritism, no mistake. Every person receives exactly what their choices have earned, and the good person receives more than they earned. For the reader, the verse is an invitation and a comfort. It invites everyone to bring whatever good they can, however small, knowing it will be returned multiplied. And it comforts the struggling believer that their sincere efforts are seen, valued, and will be repaid with a generosity that far exceeds anything they could have imagined.",
  "85": "“Most certainly, the One Who has ordained the Quran for you will ultimately bring you back home to Mecca.” This verse contains a specific promise of comfort to the Prophet at a time of great hardship. He had been forced to leave Mecca, driven out by the persecution of his people. The verse assures him that the same Lord who gave him the Quran would, in the end, return him to the city from which he had been expelled. And history confirmed this promise; some years later, the Prophet did return to Mecca in victory, entering it not as a vengeful conqueror but as a humble, merciful leader.\n\nThe connection between the two ideas in the verse is beautiful. The One who “ordained the Quran” is the One who will “bring you back home.” In other words, the God who gave him the mission is the God who will take care of his affairs along the way. The Prophet did not need to worry about the outcome. His only task was to deliver the message faithfully; the resolution of his circumstances belonged to the One who sent him. This is a recurring reassurance throughout the Quran: the messenger’s duty is to convey, and God’s promise is to handle the rest.\n\n“Say, My Lord knows best who has come with true guidance and who is clearly astray.” The verse gives the Prophet a reply to the accusations of his opponents. They claimed he was misguided and they were on the right path. He is told to leave the matter to God’s perfect knowledge. He does not need to win the argument or prove himself by their standards. His Lord knows, with complete certainty, who carries genuine guidance and who has wandered far from the truth. Time, and the Day of Judgment, will make that knowledge plain to everyone.\n\nThis verse is a source of deep strength for anyone who faces rejection while trying to do what is right. The Prophet was opposed, mocked, and driven from his home, yet he was promised that the One who gave him the message would also secure his return. The believer who holds onto the truth in difficult circumstances can take the same comfort. The outcome is not in human hands. It rests with the Lord who ordained the guidance in the first place, the Lord whose knowledge separates true guidance from clear error, and whose promise, as this very verse shows, never fails to come true.",
  "86": "“You never expected this Book to be revealed to you, but it came only as a mercy from your Lord.” This verse reminds the Prophet, and through him every reader, that the gift of revelation was entirely unearned and unexpected. Muhammad did not spend his life preparing to be a prophet, seeking the role, or imagining that a scripture would come to him. He lived as an honest merchant, respected for his character, until God, in His own time, chose him for the greatest of missions. The Quran’s arrival in his life was not the fulfilment of a personal ambition; it was an unlooked-for mercy, a gift descending from above.\n\nThe word “mercy” is key. The Quran is described here not as a burden or a duty but as a mercy. This reframes the whole of religious life. Guidance is not a heavy tax God imposes on human beings; it is a kindness He offers them, a light by which they can find their way home. To receive the Book is to receive mercy. To live by it is to walk in that mercy. Every command, every warning, every promise in the Quran is, at its root, an expression of God’s care for His creatures.\n\n“So never side with the disbelievers in their disbelief.” Flowing directly from the gift of mercy comes a clear instruction. Since the Book is a mercy, the Prophet must not compromise with those who reject it. He must not soften the message to please them, nor ally himself with their rejection out of fear or desire for acceptance. The instruction is firm because the stakes are high. The mercy that has been given must be honoured by wholehearted loyalty to it, not diluted by trying to keep a foot in both camps.\n\nThis verse speaks powerfully to a timeless temptation. There is always pressure on those who carry the truth to soften it, to accommodate it to the preferences of the age, to make peace with disbelief in exchange for comfort or approval. The verse refuses that path. The mercy of guidance is too precious to be bargained away. For the believer, the lesson is to hold the gift of faith with gratitude and with courage, never trading the clear truth for the passing favour of those who reject it, and remembering always that the Book they have been given is, before anything else, a mercy that deserves their wholehearted devotion.",
  "87": "“Do not let them turn you away from the revelations of Allah after they have been sent down to you.” This verse warns the Prophet against a subtle but real danger. The pressure to abandon the message does not always come before it is accepted; it often comes afterward. Once the revelations have been delivered, the opponents will try to push the bearer away from them, whether through bribery, threat, mockery, or the seductive promise of compromise. The Prophet is told to hold firm, to let nothing shake him from the revelations that God Himself has sent down.\n\nThe phrase “after they have been sent down to you” carries a quiet weight. The revelations are not the Prophet’s own invention to be adjusted at will. They came down from God, and therefore they carry divine authority. To be turned away from them would be to turn away from the very truth of heaven. The verse reminds the reader that scripture is a trust, and the one who carries it must guard it faithfully, neither adding to it to please people nor subtracting from it to avoid conflict.\n\n“Rather, invite all to the Way of your Lord.” Against the pressure to retreat, the verse sets a positive command: keep inviting. The way to resist being turned aside is not merely to stand still but to move forward in the work of calling people to God. The word “all” is significant. The invitation is not restricted to a tribe, a class, or a nation. It is offered to everyone, because the message of the One God is universal. The Prophet’s role was to keep that invitation open and clear, regardless of how people responded.\n\n“And never be one of the polytheists.” The verse closes with a strong, even startling, command. A prophet is told not to be among those who associate partners with God. The force of the wording shows how seriously the Quran takes any form of compromise with shirk. The lesson reaches far beyond the Prophet himself. Shirk, the association of partners with God, is not only the worship of statues. It is any sharing of the loyalty, love, and obedience that belong to God alone. The verse warns every believer that the pressure to dilute pure devotion to the One God is constant, and that the response must be equally constant: hold firmly to the revelations, keep inviting others to the Lord, and never let the heart slip into giving to anything else what belongs only to the Creator.",
  "88": "“And do not invoke any other god with Allah.” With this final verse, the chapter comes full circle. It began with the story of Pharaoh, who claimed to be a god, and with Korah, who made wealth his god. Now it closes by returning to the foundation of everything: invoke only Allah. The word “invoke” in the Quran carries the sense of worship, calling upon, and relying upon. To invoke another god alongside Allah is the practical core of every form of false religion. The command is simple, and after the long parade of stories it lands with full weight: do not call upon anything else.\n\n“There is no god worthy of worship except Him.” This is the creed restated one final time. Every other claim to divinity, from Pharaoh’s boast to the idols of Mecca, has been exposed and demolished across the chapter. What remains standing is the one truth: God alone is worthy of worship. The believer’s entire life is built on this single foundation.\n\n“Everything is bound to perish except He Himself.” This is one of the most majestic statements in the Quran. Everything, without exception, will pass away. The treasures Korah hoarded, the tower Pharaoh built, the homes people love, the earth itself that swallowed Korah, all of it is headed for extinction. Only God remains. The Arabic refers to the Face, the very Self, of God, the one Reality that does not decay. In a single sentence, the verse puts every worldly attachment in its place. Why devote the heart to what will perish, when the One who does not perish is calling?\n\n“All authority belongs to Him. And to Him you will all be returned.” The chapter ends where it began, with the sovereignty of God and the return of all creation to Him. Authority is His alone, which means every tyrant who ever claimed it was only borrowing it for a moment. And the return to Him is certain, which means every life is heading toward the meeting that gives all of this its weight.\n\nRead as a whole, this closing verse gathers up the entire chapter. The stories of arrogance and ruin, of faith and reward, of warning and mercy, all converge on this one truth: God alone is eternal, God alone is worthy, and every soul is returning to Him. The chapter opened with mysterious letters and a clear Book, and it closes with a clear call. For the reader, there could be no simpler or more searching conclusion. Turn the heart wholly toward the One who does not perish, worship Him alone, and prepare for the return, because everything else will fade, and only He will remain."
};
